[{"input":"# Lecture 3: Limits and Continuity\n\nCalculus was created to describe how quantities change:\n\n- *differentiation*, for finding the rate of change of a given function, and\n- *integration*, for finding a function having a given rate of change.\n\nBoth of these procedure are based on the fundamental concept of *limit* of a function.\n\nIn this chapter we will introduce the limit concept and develop some of its properties, including the nice behaviour of some functions that is called *continuity*.\n","output":"","result":"# Lecture 3: Limits and Continuity\n\nCalculus was created to describe how quantities change:\n\n- *differentiation*, for finding the rate of change of a given function, and\n- *integration*, for finding a function having a given rate of change.\n\nBoth of these procedure are based on the fundamental concept of *limit* of a function.\n\nIn this chapter we will introduce the limit concept and develop some of its properties, including the nice behaviour of some functions that is called *continuity*.\n","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2628-lecture-3-limits-and-continuity\">\n<h1>Lecture 3: Limits and Continuity</h1>\n<p>Calculus was created to describe how quantities change:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>differentiation</em>, for finding the rate of change of a given function, and</li>\n<li><em>integration</em>, for finding a function having a given rate of change.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both of these procedure are based on the fundamental concept of <em>limit</em> of a function.</p>\n<p>In this chapter we will introduce the limit concept and develop some of its properties, including the nice behaviour of some functions that is called <em>continuity</em>.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Average and Instantaneous Velocity\n\nThe position of a moving object is a function of time. The average velocity of the object over a time interval is found by dividing the change in the object's position by the length of the time interval.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"The average velocity of a falling rock.\"\n\n    Physical experiments show that if a rock is dropped from rest near the surface of the earth, in the first ``t\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` if will fall a distance\n\n    ```math\n    y = \\frac{gt^2}{2}\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\,,\n    ```\n\n    with ``g\\approx 9.8`` a constant representing the combined action of the gravition (from mass distribution within earth) and centrifugal forces (from the earth's rotation).\n\n    1. What is the average velocity of the falling rock during the first ``2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` (time interval ``\\left[0,2\\right]``)?\n    \n        ```math\n        \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-0^2}{2-0}=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n        ```\n\n    2. What is the average velocity of the falling rock in the time interval ``\\left[1,2\\right]``?\n    \n        ```math\n        \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-1^2}{2-1}=14.7\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n        ```\n\nThe instantaneous velocity of the object at the instant ``t`` can be estimated by evaluating the average velocity in a small time interval containing ``t``.","output":"","result":"## Average and Instantaneous Velocity\n\nThe position of a moving object is a function of time. The average velocity of the object over a time interval is found by dividing the change in the object's position by the length of the time interval.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"The average velocity of a falling rock.\"\n\n    Physical experiments show that if a rock is dropped from rest near the surface of the earth, in the first ``t\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` if will fall a distance\n\n    ```math\n    y = \\frac{gt^2}{2}\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\,,\n    ```\n\n    with ``g\\approx 9.8`` a constant representing the combined action of the gravition (from mass distribution within earth) and centrifugal forces (from the earth's rotation).\n\n    1. What is the average velocity of the falling rock during the first ``2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` (time interval ``\\left[0,2\\right]``)?\n    \n        ```math\n        \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-0^2}{2-0}=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n        ```\n\n    2. What is the average velocity of the falling rock in the time interval ``\\left[1,2\\right]``?\n    \n        ```math\n        \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-1^2}{2-1}=14.7\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n        ```\n\nThe instantaneous velocity of the object at the instant ``t`` can be estimated by evaluating the average velocity in a small time interval containing ``t``.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2629-average-and-instantaneous-velocity\">\n<h1>Average and Instantaneous Velocity</h1>\n<p>The position of a moving object is a function of time. The average velocity of the object over a time interval is found by dividing the change in the object’s position by the length of the time interval.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>The average velocity of a falling rock.</h5>\n<p>Physical experiments show that if a rock is dropped from rest near the surface of the earth, in the first <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\)</span> if will fall a distance</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\ny = \\frac{gt^2}{2}\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\approx 9.8\\)</span> a constant representing the combined action of the gravition (from mass distribution within earth) and centrifugal forces (from the earth’s rotation).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>What is the average velocity of the falling rock during the first <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\)</span> (time interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[0,2\\right]\\)</span>)?</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-0^2}{2-0}=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What is the average velocity of the falling rock in the time interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[1,2\\right]\\)</span>?</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-1^2}{2-1}=14.7\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</aside>\n<p>The instantaneous velocity of the object at the instant <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span> can be estimated by evaluating the average velocity in a small time interval containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const intervals = [\n  [1, 1.1],\n  [1, 1.01],\n  [1, 1.001],\n  [1, 1.0001],\n];\n\nconst rows = intervals.map(([x1, x2]) => {\n  const fx = 4.9 * (x2 + x1);\n  const roundedFx = String(Number(fx.toFixed(4)));\n\n  return [\n    JSCells.latex(`\\\\left[${x1},${x2}\\\\right]`),\n    JSCells.latex(roundedFx),\n  ];\n});\n\nJSCells.table({\n  headers: [\"Time Interval\", \"Average Velocity\"],\n  caption: \"How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time \\\\(t=1\\\\,\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{s}\\\\right]\\\\)?\",\n  rows,\n  align: [\"center\", \"center\"],\n});","output":"","result":"How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time t=1[s]?Time IntervalAverage Velocity[1,1.1]10.29[1,1.01]9.849[1,1.001]9.8049[1,1.0001]9.8005","resultHTML":"<figure data-type=\"table\"><figcaption>How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>?</figcaption><table><thead><tr><th class=\"center\">Time Interval</th><th class=\"center\">Average Velocity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>10.29</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.01</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>9.849</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>9.8049</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.0001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>9.8005</mn></math></span></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"const intervals = [\n  [2, 2.1],\n  [2, 2.01],\n  [2, 2.001],\n  [2, 2.0001],\n];\n\nconst rows = intervals.map(([x1, x2]) => {\n  const fx = 4.9 * (x2 + x1);\n  const roundedFx = String(Number(fx.toFixed(4)));\n\n  return [\n    JSCells.latex(`\\\\left[${x1},${x2}\\\\right]`),\n    JSCells.latex(roundedFx),\n  ];\n});\n\nJSCells.table({\n  headers: [\"Time Interval\", \"Average Velocity\"],\n  caption: \"How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time \\\\(t=2\\\\,\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{s}\\\\right]\\\\)?\",\n  rows,\n  align: [\"center\", \"center\"],\n});","output":"","result":"How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time t=2[s]?Time IntervalAverage Velocity[2,2.1]20.09[2,2.01]19.649[2,2.001]19.6049[2,2.0001]19.6005","resultHTML":"<figure data-type=\"table\"><figcaption>How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>?</figcaption><table><thead><tr><th class=\"center\">Time Interval</th><th class=\"center\">Average Velocity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>20.09</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.01</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>19.649</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>19.6049</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.0001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>19.6005</mn></math></span></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"In the second example the average velocity of the falling rock over the time interval ``\\left[t,t+h\\right]`` is\n\n```math\n\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{\\left(t+h\\right)^2-t^2}{\\left(t+h\\right)-t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\n```\n\nWe examined the values of this average velocity for time intervals whose lengths ``h`` became smaller and smaller. We were finding the *limit of the average velocity as ``h`` tends to zero*. This is expressed symbolically in the form\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\\,.\n```\n\nWe can't find the limit of the fraction by just substituting ``h=0`` because that would involve dividing by zero. However, we can calculate the limit by first performing som algebraic simplifications on the expression:\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\left(2t+h\\right)\\,.\n```\n\nThe final form no longer involves division by ``h``. It approaches ``gt + \\frac{g}{2}0=gt``. In particular, at ``t=1\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` and ``t=2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``, the instantaneous velocities are ``v_1=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]`` and ``v_2=19.6\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]``, respectively.","output":"","result":"In the second example the average velocity of the falling rock over the time interval ``\\left[t,t+h\\right]`` is\n\n```math\n\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{\\left(t+h\\right)^2-t^2}{\\left(t+h\\right)-t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\n```\n\nWe examined the values of this average velocity for time intervals whose lengths ``h`` became smaller and smaller. We were finding the *limit of the average velocity as ``h`` tends to zero*. This is expressed symbolically in the form\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\\,.\n```\n\nWe can't find the limit of the fraction by just substituting ``h=0`` because that would involve dividing by zero. However, we can calculate the limit by first performing som algebraic simplifications on the expression:\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\left(2t+h\\right)\\,.\n```\n\nThe final form no longer involves division by ``h``. It approaches ``gt + \\frac{g}{2}0=gt``. In particular, at ``t=1\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` and ``t=2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``, the instantaneous velocities are ``v_1=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]`` and ``v_2=19.6\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]``, respectively.","resultHTML":"<p>In the second example the average velocity of the falling rock over the time interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[t,t+h\\right]\\)</span> is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{\\left(t+h\\right)^2-t^2}{\\left(t+h\\right)-t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>We examined the values of this average velocity for time intervals whose lengths <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h\\)</span> became smaller and smaller. We were finding the <em>limit of the average velocity as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h\\)</span> tends to zero</em>. This is expressed symbolically in the form</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>We can’t find the limit of the fraction by just substituting <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h=0\\)</span> because that would involve dividing by zero. However, we can calculate the limit by first performing som algebraic simplifications on the expression:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\left(2t+h\\right)\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The final form no longer involves division by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h\\)</span>. It approaches <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(gt + \\frac{g}{2}0=gt\\)</span>. In particular, at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t=1\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t=2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\)</span>, the instantaneous velocities are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(v_1=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(v_2=19.6\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\)</span>, respectively.</p>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The Area of a Circle\n\nAll circles are similar geometric figures; they all have the same shape and differ only in size. The ratio of the circumference ``C`` to the diameter ``2r`` has the same value for all circles. The number ``\\uppi `` is defined to be this common ratio:\n\n```math\n\\frac{C}{2r}=\\uppi \\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad C=2\\uppi r\\,.\n```\n\nWe were taught that the area ``A`` of a circle is this same number ``\\uppi `` times the square of the radius:\n\n```math\nA=\\uppi r^2\\,.\n```\n\nCan we deduce this area formula from the formula for the circumference?\n\nThe answer to this question lies in regarding the circle as a \"limit\" of regular polygons, which are in turn made up of triangles.\n\nSuppose a regular polygon having ``n`` sides is inscribed in a circle of radius ``r``.","output":"","result":"## The Area of a Circle\n\nAll circles are similar geometric figures; they all have the same shape and differ only in size. The ratio of the circumference ``C`` to the diameter ``2r`` has the same value for all circles. The number ``\\uppi `` is defined to be this common ratio:\n\n```math\n\\frac{C}{2r}=\\uppi \\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad C=2\\uppi r\\,.\n```\n\nWe were taught that the area ``A`` of a circle is this same number ``\\uppi `` times the square of the radius:\n\n```math\nA=\\uppi r^2\\,.\n```\n\nCan we deduce this area formula from the formula for the circumference?\n\nThe answer to this question lies in regarding the circle as a \"limit\" of regular polygons, which are in turn made up of triangles.\n\nSuppose a regular polygon having ``n`` sides is inscribed in a circle of radius ``r``.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2634-the-area-of-a-circle\">\n<h1>The Area of a Circle</h1>\n<p>All circles are similar geometric figures; they all have the same shape and differ only in size. The ratio of the circumference <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C\\)</span> to the diameter <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2r\\)</span> has the same value for all circles. The number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\uppi \\)</span> is defined to be this common ratio:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\frac{C}{2r}=\\uppi \\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad C=2\\uppi r\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>We were taught that the area <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span> of a circle is this same number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\uppi \\)</span> times the square of the radius:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=\\uppi r^2\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Can we deduce this area formula from the formula for the circumference?</p>\n<p>The answer to this question lies in regarding the circle as a “limit” of regular polygons, which are in turn made up of triangles.</p>\n<p>Suppose a regular polygon having <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> sides is inscribed in a circle of radius <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const width = 320;\nconst height = 302;\nconst r = 150;\nconst xmid = 151;\nconst ymid = 151;\n\n// Approximate label dimensions for the LaTeX labels.\nconst fontX = 16;\nconst fontY = 24;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure(\n  { width, height },\n  \"A regular polygon (green) of n sides inscribed in a red circle. Here n = 9.\"\n);\n\nfigure.circle()\n  .cx(xmid).cy(ymid).r(r)\n  .fill(\"none\").stroke(\"#ff0000\").strokeWidth(1);\n\nconst points = Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => {\n  const t = (2 * Math.PI * i) / 9;\n  return [xmid + r * Math.cos(t), ymid + r * Math.sin(t)];\n});\n\nfor (let i = 0; i < 9; i += 1) {\n  figure.line()\n    .x1(points[i][0]).y1(points[i][1])\n    .x2(points[i + 1][0]).y2(points[i + 1][1])\n    .stroke(\"#008000\").strokeWidth(1);\n}\n\nconst p1 = points[0];\nconst p9 = points[8];\nconst midpoint = [(p1[0] + p9[0]) / 2, (p1[1] + p9[1]) / 2];\n\nfigure.line()\n  .x1(xmid).y1(xmid).x2(p1[0]).y2(p1[1])\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").strokeWidth(1);\n\nfigure.line()\n  .x1(xmid).y1(xmid).x2(p9[0]).y2(p9[1])\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").strokeWidth(1);\n\nfigure.line()\n  .x1(xmid).y1(xmid).x2(midpoint[0]).y2(midpoint[1])\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").strokeWidth(1)\n  .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", {\n  x: xmid - fontX,\n  y: ymid - 0.5 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"r\", {\n  x: xmid + 0.5 * r - 0.5 * fontX,\n  y: ymid,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"A\", {\n  x: 305,\n  y: ymid - 0.5 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"B\", {\n  x: p9[0] + 0.25 * fontX,\n  y: p9[1] - 0.75 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"M\", {\n  x: midpoint[0] - 1.5 * fontX,\n  y: midpoint[1] - 0.75 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\n\nconst arcEnd = [\n  75 * Math.cos(-Math.PI / 9) + xmid + 1.5,\n  75 * Math.sin(-Math.PI / 9) + ymid + 1.75,\n];\n\nfigure.path()\n  .d(`M 226 151 A 75 75 0 0 0 ${arcEnd[0]} ${arcEnd[1]}`)\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").fill(\"none\");\n\n// Draw the arrowhead as a polygon because the SVG helper doesn't expose markers.\nconst direction = [-Math.sin(Math.PI / 9), -Math.cos(Math.PI / 9)];\nconst perpendicular = [-direction[1], direction[0]];\nconst arrowLength = 6;\nconst arrowHalfWidth = 3;\nconst arrowBase = [\n  arcEnd[0] - arrowLength * direction[0],\n  arcEnd[1] - arrowLength * direction[1],\n];\n\nfigure.polygon().points([\n  arcEnd,\n  [\n    arrowBase[0] + arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[0],\n    arrowBase[1] + arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[1],\n  ],\n  [\n    arrowBase[0] - arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[0],\n    arrowBase[1] - arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[1],\n  ],\n].map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`).join(\" \"))\n  .fill(\"#000000\");\n\nfigure.label(String.raw`\\frac{\\pi}{n}`, {\n  x: xmid + 80 * Math.cos(-Math.PI / 18),\n  y: ymid + 80 * Math.sin(-Math.PI / 9),\n  width: fontX,\n  height: 2 * fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"OrABMπnA regular polygon (green) of n sides inscribed in a red circle. Here n = 9.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"320\" height=\"302\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 302\"><circle cx=\"151\" cy=\"151\" r=\"150\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></circle><line x1=\"301\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"265.9066664678467\" y2=\"247.41814145298088\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"265.9066664678467\" y1=\"247.41814145298088\" x2=\"177.04722665003956\" y2=\"298.72116295183116\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"177.04722665003956\" y1=\"298.72116295183116\" x2=\"76.00000000000003\" y2=\"280.9038105676658\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"76.00000000000003\" y1=\"280.9038105676658\" x2=\"10.046106882113747\" y2=\"202.30302149885034\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"10.046106882113747\" y1=\"202.30302149885034\" x2=\"10.046106882113747\" y2=\"99.6969785011497\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"10.046106882113747\" y1=\"99.6969785011497\" x2=\"75.99999999999993\" y2=\"21.096189432334228\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"75.99999999999993\" y1=\"21.096189432334228\" x2=\"177.0472266500395\" y2=\"3.278837048168782\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"177.0472266500395\" y1=\"3.278837048168782\" x2=\"265.90666646784666\" y2=\"54.58185854701907\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"265.90666646784666\" y1=\"54.58185854701907\" x2=\"301\" y2=\"150.99999999999997\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"151\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"301\" y2=\"151\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"151\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"265.90666646784666\" y2=\"54.58185854701907\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"151\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"283.45333323392333\" y2=\"102.79092927350953\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><foreignObject x=\"135\" y=\"139\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"218\" y=\"151\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"305\" y=\"139\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"269.90666646784666\" y=\"36.58185854701907\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>B</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"259.45333323392333\" y=\"84.79092927350953\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>M</mi></math></div></foreignObject><path d=\"M 226 151 A 75 75 0 0 0 222.97694655894313 127.09848925057484\" stroke=\"#000000\" fill=\"none\"></path><polygon points=\"222.97694655894313,127.09848925057484 227.84814528125486,131.71058454531328 222.20998955653943,133.7627054052673\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"229.78462024097664\" y=\"123.63838853394651\" width=\"16\" height=\"48\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption>A regular polygon (green) of n sides inscribed in a red circle. Here n = 9.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"The perimeter ``P_n`` and the area ``A_n`` of the polygon are, respectively, less than the circumference ``C`` and the area ``A`` of the circle, but if ``n`` is large ``P_n`` is close to ``C`` and ``A_n`` is close to ``A``. We could expect ``P_n`` to approach the limit ``C`` and ``A_n`` to approach the limit ``A`` as ``n`` tends to infinity.\n\nSince the total angle around the point ``O`` is ``2\\uppi `` radians, ``\\angle AOB`` is ``\\frac{2\\uppi }{n}`` radians. If ``M`` is the midpoint of ``AB``, then ``O`` bisects ``\\angle AOB``. We can write the length of ``AB`` and the area of ``\\triangle OAB`` in terms of the radius:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|AB\\right|=&2\\left|AM\\right|=2r\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\n\\triangle OAB=&\\frac{1}{2}\\left|AB\\right|\\left|OM\\right|=r^2\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nThe perimeter ``P_n`` and area ``A_n`` of the polygon are ``n`` times these expressions:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_n=&2rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\nA_n=&r^2n\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nSolving the first equation for ``rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{P_n}{2}`` and substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n```math\nA_n = \\frac{1}{2}P_nr\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n```\n\nNow ``\\angle AOM=\\frac{\\uppi}{n}`` approaches ``0`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, so its cosine, ``\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{\\left|OM\\right|}{\\left|OA\\right|}``, approaches ``1``. Since ``P_n`` approaches ``C=2\\uppi r`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, the expression for ``A_n`` approaches ``\\frac{1}{2}\\left(2\\uppi r\\right)r\\left(1\\right)=\\uppi r^2``, which must therefore be the area of the circle.\n\n","output":"","result":"The perimeter ``P_n`` and the area ``A_n`` of the polygon are, respectively, less than the circumference ``C`` and the area ``A`` of the circle, but if ``n`` is large ``P_n`` is close to ``C`` and ``A_n`` is close to ``A``. We could expect ``P_n`` to approach the limit ``C`` and ``A_n`` to approach the limit ``A`` as ``n`` tends to infinity.\n\nSince the total angle around the point ``O`` is ``2\\uppi `` radians, ``\\angle AOB`` is ``\\frac{2\\uppi }{n}`` radians. If ``M`` is the midpoint of ``AB``, then ``O`` bisects ``\\angle AOB``. We can write the length of ``AB`` and the area of ``\\triangle OAB`` in terms of the radius:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|AB\\right|=&2\\left|AM\\right|=2r\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\n\\triangle OAB=&\\frac{1}{2}\\left|AB\\right|\\left|OM\\right|=r^2\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nThe perimeter ``P_n`` and area ``A_n`` of the polygon are ``n`` times these expressions:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_n=&2rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\nA_n=&r^2n\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nSolving the first equation for ``rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{P_n}{2}`` and substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n```math\nA_n = \\frac{1}{2}P_nr\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n```\n\nNow ``\\angle AOM=\\frac{\\uppi}{n}`` approaches ``0`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, so its cosine, ``\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{\\left|OM\\right|}{\\left|OA\\right|}``, approaches ``1``. Since ``P_n`` approaches ``C=2\\uppi r`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, the expression for ``A_n`` approaches ``\\frac{1}{2}\\left(2\\uppi r\\right)r\\left(1\\right)=\\uppi r^2``, which must therefore be the area of the circle.\n\n","resultHTML":"<p>The perimeter <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P_n\\)</span> and the area <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_n\\)</span> of the polygon are, respectively, less than the circumference <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C\\)</span> and the area <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span> of the circle, but if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> is large <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P_n\\)</span> is close to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_n\\)</span> is close to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span>. We could expect <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P_n\\)</span> to approach the limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_n\\)</span> to approach the limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> tends to infinity.</p>\n<p>Since the total angle around the point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(O\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2\\uppi \\)</span> radians, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\angle AOB\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{2\\uppi }{n}\\)</span> radians. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M\\)</span> is the midpoint of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(AB\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(O\\)</span> bisects <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\angle AOB\\)</span>. We can write the length of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(AB\\)</span> and the area of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\triangle OAB\\)</span> in terms of the radius:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|AB\\right|=&amp;2\\left|AM\\right|=2r\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\n\\triangle OAB=&amp;\\frac{1}{2}\\left|AB\\right|\\left|OM\\right|=r^2\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The perimeter <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P_n\\)</span> and area <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_n\\)</span> of the polygon are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> times these expressions:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_n=&amp;2rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\nA_n=&amp;r^2n\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Solving the first equation for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{P_n}{2}\\)</span> and substituting into the second equation, we get</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA_n = \\frac{1}{2}P_nr\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Now <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\angle AOM=\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\)</span> approaches <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> tends to infinity, so its cosine, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{\\left|OM\\right|}{\\left|OA\\right|}\\)</span>, approaches <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1\\)</span>. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P_n\\)</span> approaches <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C=2\\uppi r\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> tends to infinity, the expression for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_n\\)</span> approaches <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\left(2\\uppi r\\right)r\\left(1\\right)=\\uppi r^2\\)</span>, which must therefore be the area of the circle.</p>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Limits Defined\n\nIn order to speak meaningfully about rate of change, tangent lines, and areas bounded by curves, we have to investigate the process of finding limits. Let us look at some examples.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Describe the behaviour of the function ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}`` near ``x=1``.\"\n\n    Note that ``f(x)`` is defined for all real numbers ``x`` except ``x=1``. (We can't divide by zero.) For any ``x\\ne1`` we can simplify the expression for ``f\\left(x\\right)`` by factoring the numerator and cancelling common factors:\n\n    ```math\n    f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{\\left(x-1\\right)\\left(x+1\\right)}{x-1}=x+1\\quad\\textrm{for }x\\ne1\\,.\n    ```\n\n    The graph of ``f`` is the line ``y=x+1`` with one point removed, namely, the point ``\\left(1,2\\right)``.\n    \n    \n\tThis removed point is shown as a *hole* in the graph. Even though ``\\left(1\\right)`` is not defined, it is clear that we can make the value of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` *as close as we want* to ``2`` by choosing ``x`` *close enough* to ``1``. Therefore, we say ``f`` approaches the limit ``2`` as ``x`` tends to ``1``. We write this as\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to1}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}=2\\,.\n    ```","output":"","result":"## Limits Defined\n\nIn order to speak meaningfully about rate of change, tangent lines, and areas bounded by curves, we have to investigate the process of finding limits. Let us look at some examples.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Describe the behaviour of the function ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}`` near ``x=1``.\"\n\n    Note that ``f(x)`` is defined for all real numbers ``x`` except ``x=1``. (We can't divide by zero.) For any ``x\\ne1`` we can simplify the expression for ``f\\left(x\\right)`` by factoring the numerator and cancelling common factors:\n\n    ```math\n    f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{\\left(x-1\\right)\\left(x+1\\right)}{x-1}=x+1\\quad\\textrm{for }x\\ne1\\,.\n    ```\n\n    The graph of ``f`` is the line ``y=x+1`` with one point removed, namely, the point ``\\left(1,2\\right)``.\n    \n    \n\tThis removed point is shown as a *hole* in the graph. Even though ``\\left(1\\right)`` is not defined, it is clear that we can make the value of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` *as close as we want* to ``2`` by choosing ``x`` *close enough* to ``1``. Therefore, we say ``f`` approaches the limit ``2`` as ``x`` tends to ``1``. We write this as\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to1}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}=2\\,.\n    ```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2637-limits-defined\">\n<h1>Limits Defined</h1>\n<p>In order to speak meaningfully about rate of change, tangent lines, and areas bounded by curves, we have to investigate the process of finding limits. Let us look at some examples.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Describe the behaviour of the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}\\)</span> near <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>.</h5>\n<p>Note that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f(x)\\)</span> is defined for all real numbers <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> except <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>. (We can’t divide by zero.) For any <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\ne1\\)</span> we can simplify the expression for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> by factoring the numerator and cancelling common factors:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nf\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{\\left(x-1\\right)\\left(x+1\\right)}{x-1}=x+1\\quad\\textrm{for }x\\ne1\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The graph of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is the line <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=x+1\\)</span> with one point removed, namely, the point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(1,2\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>This removed point is shown as a <em>hole</em> in the graph. Even though <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(1\\right)\\)</span> is not defined, it is clear that we can make the value of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> <em>as close as we want</em> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2\\)</span> by choosing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> <em>close enough</em> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1\\)</span>. Therefore, we say <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> approaches the limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> tends to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1\\)</span>. We write this as</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to1}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}=2\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</aside>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 40;\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 7 * scale,\n    height: 6 * scale,\n    caption: \"The graph of \\\\(f(x) = (x^2 - 1) / (x - 1)\\\\).\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [2 * scale, 5 * scale],\n    scale,\n    x: { ticks: [1] },\n    y: { ticks: [2] },\n});\n\nconst f = (x) => (x * x - 1) / (x - 1);\n\n// Split the plot around x = 1, where f is undefined.\nfigure.plot(f, {\n    domain: [-2, 0.99],\n    samples: 301,\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\nfigure.plot(f, {\n    domain: [1.01, 5],\n    samples: 401,\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\n\nfigure.point(1, 2, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"hsl(39, 100%, 90%)\",\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy12The graph of f(x)=(x2−1)/(x−1).","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"280\" height=\"240\" vi