78.404,-38.404000000000025 278.803,-38.803 279.202,-39.202 279.601,-39.601 280,-40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"120\" r=\"3\" fill=\"hsl(39, 100%, 90%)\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle></svg><figcaption>The graph of <math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"!!! example \"What happens to the function ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\left(1+x^2\\right)^\\frac{1}{x^2}`` as ``x`` approaches zero?\"\n\n    Note that ``g`` is not defined at ``x=0``. In fact, for the moment it does not appear to be defined for any ``x`` whose square ``x^2`` is not a rational number. Let us ignore for now the problem of deciding what ``g\\left(x\\right)`` means if ``x^2`` is irrational and consider only rational values of ``x``. There is no obvious way to simplify the expression for ``g\\left(x\\right)`` as we did in previous example. However, we can use a scientific calculator to obtain approximate values of ``g\\left(x\\right)`` for some rational.\n    \n    The values of ``g\\left(x\\right)`` seem to be approaching a certain number, ``2.71828\\dots``, as ``x`` tends to ``0``. We will see in Chapter 5 that\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to 0}g\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(1+x^2\\right)^\\frac{1}{x^2}=\\mathcal{e}=2.718281828459045\\dots\\,.\n    ```","output":"","result":"!!! example \"What happens to the function ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\left(1+x^2\\right)^\\frac{1}{x^2}`` as ``x`` approaches zero?\"\n\n    Note that ``g`` is not defined at ``x=0``. In fact, for the moment it does not appear to be defined for any ``x`` whose square ``x^2`` is not a rational number. Let us ignore for now the problem of deciding what ``g\\left(x\\right)`` means if ``x^2`` is irrational and consider only rational values of ``x``. There is no obvious way to simplify the expression for ``g\\left(x\\right)`` as we did in previous example. However, we can use a scientific calculator to obtain approximate values of ``g\\left(x\\right)`` for some rational.\n    \n    The values of ``g\\left(x\\right)`` seem to be approaching a certain number, ``2.71828\\dots``, as ``x`` tends to ``0``. We will see in Chapter 5 that\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to 0}g\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(1+x^2\\right)^\\frac{1}{x^2}=\\mathcal{e}=2.718281828459045\\dots\\,.\n    ```","resultHTML":"<div data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>What happens to the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)=\\left(1+x^2\\right)^\\frac{1}{x^2}\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> approaches zero?</h5>\n<p>Note that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> is not defined at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=0\\)</span>. In fact, for the moment it does not appear to be defined for any <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> whose square <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x^2\\)</span> is not a rational number. Let us ignore for now the problem of deciding what <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> means if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x^2\\)</span> is irrational and consider only rational values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>. There is no obvious way to simplify the expression for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> as we did in previous example. However, we can use a scientific calculator to obtain approximate values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> for some rational.</p>\n<p>The values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> seem to be approaching a certain number, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2.71828\\dots\\)</span>, as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> tends to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span>. We will see in Chapter 5 that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}g\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(1+x^2\\right)^\\frac{1}{x^2}=\\mathcal{e}=2.718281828459045\\dots\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const g = (x) => (1 + x ** 2) ** (1 / x ** 2);\nconst xs = [0.1, 0.01, 0.001, 0.0001, 0.00001];\n\nJSCells.table({\n\tcaption: \"What happens to the function \\\\(g\\\\left(x\\\\right)=\\\\left(1+x^2\\\\right)^\\\\frac{1}{x^2}\\\\) as \\\\(x\\\\) approaches zero?\",\n    headers: [\n        JSCells.latex(\"x\"),\n        JSCells.latex(String.raw`g\\left(x\\right)`),\n    ],\n    rows: xs.map((x) => [\n        JSCells.latex(String.raw`\\pm ${x}`),\n        JSCells.latex(String(Number(g(x).toFixed(9)))),\n    ]),\n    align: [\"center\", \"center\"],\n});","output":"","result":"What happens to the function g(x)=(1+x2)1x2 as x approaches zero?xg(x)±0.12.704813829±0.012.718145927±0.0012.718280469±0.00012.718281798±0.000012.718282053","resultHTML":"<figure data-type=\"table\"><figcaption>What happens to the function <math><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn scriptlevel=\"2\" style=\"math-depth:2;\">1</mn><msup scriptlevel=\"2\" style=\"math-depth:2;\"><mi>x</mi><mn scriptlevel=\"2\" class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mfrac></msup></mrow></math> as <math><mi>x</mi></math> approaches zero?</figcaption><table><thead><tr><th class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span></th><th class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">±</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>2.704813829</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>2.718145927</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">±</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>2.718280469</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">±</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>2.718281798</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">±</mo><mn>0.00001</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>2.718282053</mn></math></span></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"The examples and the previous sections suggest the following informal definition of limit.\n\n::: definition \"Informal limit.\"\nIf ``f`` is defined for all ``x`` near ``a``, except possibly at ``a`` itself, and if we can ensure that ``f\\left(x\\right)`` is as close as we want to ``L`` by taking ``x`` close enough to ``a``, but not equal to ``a``, we say that the function ``f`` approaches the *limit* ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, and we write\n    \n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n```\n:::\n\nThis definition is informal because phrases such as *close as we want* and *close enough* are imprecise; their meaning depends on the context. If we want to prove results about limits a more precise definition is needed. This precise definition is based on the idea of controlling the input ``x`` of a function ``f`` so that the output ``f\\left(x\\right)`` will lie in a specific interval.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"The area of a circular disk of radius ``r\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}\\right]`` is ``A=\\uppi r^2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]``. A machinist is required to manufacture a circular metal disk having area ``400\\uppi\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]`` within an error tolerance of ``\\pm 5\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]``. How close to ``20\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}\\right]`` must the machinist control the radius of the disk to achieve this?\"\n\n    The machinist wants ``\\left|\\uppi r^2-400\\uppi\\right|<5``, that is,\n\n    ```math\n    400\\uppi-5<\\uppi r^2<400\\uppi+5\n    ```\n\n    or, equivalently,\n\n    ```math\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sqrt{400-\\frac{5}{\\uppi}}<&r<\\sqrt{400+\\frac{5}{\\uppi}}\\\\\n        19.96017<&r<20.03975\\,.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    ```\n\n    Thus, the machinist needs ``\\left|r-20\\right|<0.03975``.\n\nWhen we say that ``f`` has limit ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, we are really saying that we can ensure that the error ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|`` will be less than *any* allowed tolerance, no matter how small, by taking ``x`` *close enough* to ``a`` (but not equal to ``a``). It is traditional to use ``\\varepsilon``, the Greek letter \"epsilon\", for the size of the allowable *error* and ``\\delta``, the Greek letter \"delta\" for the difference ``\\left|x-a\\right|`` that measures how close ``x`` must be to ``a`` to ensure that the error is within that tolerance.\n","output":"","result":"The examples and the previous sections suggest the following informal definition of limit.\n\n::: definition \"Informal limit.\"\nIf ``f`` is defined for all ``x`` near ``a``, except possibly at ``a`` itself, and if we can ensure that ``f\\left(x\\right)`` is as close as we want to ``L`` by taking ``x`` close enough to ``a``, but not equal to ``a``, we say that the function ``f`` approaches the *limit* ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, and we write\n    \n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n```\n:::\n\nThis definition is informal because phrases such as *close as we want* and *close enough* are imprecise; their meaning depends on the context. If we want to prove results about limits a more precise definition is needed. This precise definition is based on the idea of controlling the input ``x`` of a function ``f`` so that the output ``f\\left(x\\right)`` will lie in a specific interval.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"The area of a circular disk of radius ``r\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}\\right]`` is ``A=\\uppi r^2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]``. A machinist is required to manufacture a circular metal disk having area ``400\\uppi\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]`` within an error tolerance of ``\\pm 5\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]``. How close to ``20\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}\\right]`` must the machinist control the radius of the disk to achieve this?\"\n\n    The machinist wants ``\\left|\\uppi r^2-400\\uppi\\right|<5``, that is,\n\n    ```math\n    400\\uppi-5<\\uppi r^2<400\\uppi+5\n    ```\n\n    or, equivalently,\n\n    ```math\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sqrt{400-\\frac{5}{\\uppi}}<&r<\\sqrt{400+\\frac{5}{\\uppi}}\\\\\n        19.96017<&r<20.03975\\,.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    ```\n\n    Thus, the machinist needs ``\\left|r-20\\right|<0.03975``.\n\nWhen we say that ``f`` has limit ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, we are really saying that we can ensure that the error ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|`` will be less than *any* allowed tolerance, no matter how small, by taking ``x`` *close enough* to ``a`` (but not equal to ``a``). It is traditional to use ``\\varepsilon``, the Greek letter \"epsilon\", for the size of the allowable *error* and ``\\delta``, the Greek letter \"delta\" for the difference ``\\left|x-a\\right|`` that measures how close ``x`` must be to ``a`` to ensure that the error is within that tolerance.\n","resultHTML":"<p>The examples and the previous sections suggest the following informal definition of limit.</p>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<h5>Informal limit.</h5>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is defined for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> near <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, except possibly at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> itself, and if we can ensure that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> is as close as we want to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> by taking <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> close enough to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, but not equal to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, we say that the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> approaches the <em>limit</em> <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> tends to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, and we write</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>This definition is informal because phrases such as <em>close as we want</em> and <em>close enough</em> are imprecise; their meaning depends on the context. If we want to prove results about limits a more precise definition is needed. This precise definition is based on the idea of controlling the input <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> of a function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> so that the output <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> will lie in a specific interval.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>The area of a circular disk of radius <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}\\right]\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=\\uppi r^2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]\\)</span>. A machinist is required to manufacture a circular metal disk having area <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(400\\uppi\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]\\)</span> within an error tolerance of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\pm 5\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}^2\\right]\\)</span>. How close to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(20\\,\\left[\\mathrm{cm}\\right]\\)</span> must the machinist control the radius of the disk to achieve this?</h5>\n<p>The machinist wants <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|\\uppi r^2-400\\uppi\\right|&lt;5\\)</span>, that is,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n400\\uppi-5&lt;\\uppi r^2&lt;400\\uppi+5\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>or, equivalently,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sqrt{400-\\frac{5}{\\uppi}}&lt;&amp;r&lt;\\sqrt{400+\\frac{5}{\\uppi}}\\\\\n    19.96017&lt;&amp;r&lt;20.03975\\,.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus, the machinist needs <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|r-20\\right|&lt;0.03975\\)</span>.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>When we say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> tends to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, we are really saying that we can ensure that the error <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\)</span> will be less than <em>any</em> allowed tolerance, no matter how small, by taking <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> <em>close enough</em> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> (but not equal to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>). It is traditional to use <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)</span>, the Greek letter “epsilon”, for the size of the allowable <em>error</em> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span>, the Greek letter “delta” for the difference <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|\\)</span> that measures how close <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> must be to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> to ensure that the error is within that tolerance.</p>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 40;\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 6 * scale,\n    height: 5 * scale,\n    caption: \"If \\\\(x ≠ a\\\\) and \\\\(|x - a| < δ\\\\), then \\\\(|f(x) - L| < ε\\\\).\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [scale+10, 4 * scale],\n    scale,\n    x: {\n        ticks: [\n            { value: 2.4, label: String.raw`a-\\delta`, length: 3 },\n            { value: 3, label: \"a\", length: 1 },\n            { value: 3.6, label: String.raw`\\ a+\\delta`, length: 3 },\n        ],\n    },\n    y: {\n        ticks: [\n            { value: 3, label: String.raw`L+\\varepsilon`, length: 4 },\n            { value: 2, label: \"L\", length: 2 },\n            { value: 1, label: String.raw`L-\\varepsilon`, length: 4 },\n        ],\n    },\n});\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(4 * scale+10).y1(4 * scale)\n    .x2(4 * scale+10).y2(2 * scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(scale+10).y1(2 * scale)\n    .x2(4 * scale+10).y2(2 * scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.plot(\n    (x) => 0.25 * (x - 3 + Math.sqrt(7)) ** 2 + 0.25,\n    { domain: [-1, 5], samples: 601, stroke: \"#ff0000\" },\n);\n\nfigure.point(3, 2, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"#ffffff\",\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\n\nfigure.rect()\n    .x(3.4 * scale+10).y(0)\n    .width(1.2 * scale).height(4 * scale)\n    .fill(\"hsla(189, 100%, 50%, 10%)\");\n\nfigure.rect()\n    .x(scale+10).y(scale)\n    .width(4 * scale).height(2 * scale)\n    .fill(\"hsla(180, 100%, 25%, 10%)\");\n\nfigure.math().x(2.8 * scale).y(0.4 * scale).width(320).height(80).fill(\"#ff0000\").latex(String.raw`y = f(x)`);\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xya−δa a+δL+εLL−εy=f(x)If x≠a and |x−a|<δ, then |f(x)−L|<ε.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"240\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 200\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"240,160 233,156 233,164\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"50,0 46,7 54,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"226\" y=\"130\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"58\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"146\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"146\" y2=\"164\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"125\" y=\"168\" width=\"42\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"164\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"163\" y=\"168\" width=\"14\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"194\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"194\" y2=\"164\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"173\" y=\"168\" width=\"42\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"54\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"-6\" y=\"28\" width=\"56\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"54\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"22\" y=\"68\" width=\"28\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"54\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"-6\" y=\"108\" width=\"56\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><polyline points=\"10,131.66010488516724 10.399999999999999,131.9299546229543 10.799999999999997,132.19780436074143 11.200000000000003,132.4636540985285 11.600000000000001,132.72750383631558 12,132.98935357410267 12.400000000000006,133.24920331188974 12.800000000000004,133.50705304967684 13.199999999999996,133.7629027874639 13.600000000000001,134.01675252525098 14,134.26860226303808 14.399999999999999,134.51845200082514 14.799999999999997,134.76630173861224 15.200000000000003,135.0121514763993 15.600000000000001,135.2560012141864 16,135.49785095197348 16.4,135.73770068976057 16.800000000000004,135.97555042754763 17.199999999999996,136.2114001653347 17.599999999999994,136.4452499031218 18,136.6770996409089 18