23169809 225.2,-12.066736185382723 225.6,-12.87288644759559 226,-13.68103670980858 226.4,-14.491186972021495 226.8,-15.303337234234391 227.2,-16.117487496447296 227.60000000000002,-16.933637758660268 228,-17.751788020873136 228.4,-18.57193828308607 228.79999999999998,-19.394088545298928 229.20000000000002,-20.21823880751191 229.60000000000002,-21.044389069724843 230,-21.87253933193773 230.39999999999998,-22.702689594150627 230.79999999999998,-23.534839856363533 231.20000000000002,-24.368990118576505 231.6,-25.205140380789402 232,-26.043290643002308 232.39999999999998,-26.883440905215224 232.8,-27.725591167428206 233.2,-28.569741429641084 233.6,-29.41589169185397 234,-30.26404195406687 234.4,-31.11419221627986 234.8,-31.96634247849275 235.2,-32.820492740705674 235.6,-33.67664300291858 236,-34.534793265131555 236.4,-35.394943527344424 236.8,-36.25709378955733 237.2,-37.12124405177022 237.60000000000002,-37.9873943139832 238,-38.85554457619611 238.4,-39.725694838409 238.79999999999998,-40.597845100621896 239.20000000000002,-41.47199536283489 239.60000000000002,-42.34814562504778 240,-43.226295887260704 240.39999999999998,-44.106446149473584 240.79999999999998,-44.98859641168647 241.20000000000002,-45.87274667389946 241.6,-46.758896936112365 242,-47.647047198325254 242.39999999999998,-48.53719746053815 242.8,-49.42934772275112 243.2,-50.323497984964035 243.6,-51.219648247176934 244,-52.117798509389814 244.4,-53.01794877160279 244.8,-53.92009903381569 245.2,-54.8242492960286 245.6,-55.730399558241515 246,-56.6385498204545 246.4,-57.54870008266738 246.8,-58.46085034488027 247.2,-59.37500060709317 247.60000000000002,-60.291150869306165 248,-61.209301131519055 248.4,-62.12945139373198 248.79999999999998,-63.051601655944864 249.20000000000002,-63.97575191815781 249.60000000000002,-64.90190218037074 250,-65.83005244258362\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"80\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><rect x=\"146\" y=\"0\" width=\"48\" height=\"160\" fill=\"hsla(189, 100%, 50%, 10%)\"></rect><rect x=\"50\" y=\"40\" width=\"160\" height=\"80\" fill=\"hsla(180, 100%, 25%, 10%)\"></rect><foreignObject x=\"112\" y=\"16\" width=\"320\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#ff0000\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption>If <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mi>|</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>|</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math>, then <math><mrow><mi>|</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>|</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"If ``\\varepsilon`` is any strict positive number, *no matter how small*, we must be able to ensure that ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon`` by restricting ``x`` to be *close enough* to (but not equal to) ``a``. How close is close enough? It is sufficient that the distance ``\\left|x-a\\right|`` from ``x`` to ``a`` be less than a positive number ``\\delta`` that depends on ``\\varepsilon``.\n\n::: definition \"Formal Definition.\"\nWe say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` approaches the limit ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, and we write\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n```\n\nif the following condition is satisfied:\n\n```math\n\\forall\\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n:::\n\nNote the possible dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\nThe formal definition of limit does not tell you how to find the limit of a function, but it does enable you to verify that a suspected limit is correct.\n\n!!! example \"Verify that:\"\n\n    1.  ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``.\n\n        Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n        ```math\n        0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x-a\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n        ```\n\n        Clearly, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``\n\n    2.  ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c`` (``c`` is a constant).\n\n        Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n        ```math\n        0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|c-c\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n        ```\n\n        Since ``c-c=0``, we can use any positive number for ``\\delta`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c``.\n\n    3.  ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\n        Here ``a=2`` and ``L=4``. Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n        ```math\n        0<\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x^2-4\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n        ```\n\n        Now,\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|x^2-4\\right|=\\left|\\left(x+2\\right)\\left(x-2\\right)\\right|=\\left|x+2\\right|\\left|x-2\\right|\\,.\n        ```\n\n        We want the expression above to be less than ``\\varepsilon``. We can make the factor ``\\left|x-2\\right|`` as small as we wish by choosing ``\\delta`` properly, but we need to control the factor ``\\left|x+2\\right|`` so that it does not become too large.\n\n        If we first assume ``\\delta\\le1`` and require that ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta``, then we have\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|x-2\\right|<1 \\implies 1<x<3 \\implies 3<x+2<5 \\implies \\left|x+2\\right|<5\n        ```\n\n        Hence,\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-4\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\le1\\,.\n        ```\n\n        But ``5\\left|x-2\\right|<\\varepsilon`` if ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``. Therefore, if we take ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace1,\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}\\right\\rbrace``, the *minimum* of the two numbers ``1`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``, then\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|f\\left(x-4\\right)\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|<5\\times\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}=\\varepsilon\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\,.\n        ```\n\n        This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\nWe do not usually rely on the formal definition of limit to verify specific limits such as those in the last example. Rather, we appeal to general theorems about limits in particular the theorems of the next section.\n\nIf a function has a limit at a point, this limit is *unique*.\n\n::: proposition\nIf ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, then ``L=M``.\n:::\n\n::: proof \"by contradiction\"\nSuppose ``L\\ne M``.\n\nLet ``\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}>0``. \n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, there exists ``\\delta_1 > 0`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\n```\n\nand since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, there exists ``\\delta_2>0`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nLet ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon``. Therefore,\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|L-M\\right|=&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\\\le&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\<&2\\varepsilon=2\\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}=\\left|L-M\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nby the triangle inequality.\n\nThis is a contradiction, so ``L=M``.\n:::\n\nAlthough a function ``f`` can only have one limit at any particular point, it is, nevertheless, useful to be able to describe the behaviour of functions that approach different numbers a ``x`` tends to ``a`` from one side or the other.","output":"","result":"If ``\\varepsilon`` is any strict positive number, *no matter how small*, we must be able to ensure that ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon`` by restricting ``x`` to be *close enough* to (but not equal to) ``a``. How close is close enough? It is sufficient that the distance ``\\left|x-a\\right|`` from ``x`` to ``a`` be less than a positive number ``\\delta`` that depends on ``\\varepsilon``.\n\n::: definition \"Formal Definition.\"\nWe say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` approaches the limit ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, and we write\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n```\n\nif the following condition is satisfied:\n\n```math\n\\forall\\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n:::\n\nNote the possible dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\nThe formal definition of limit does not tell you how to find the limit of a function, but it does enable you to verify that a suspected limit is correct.\n\n!!! example \"Verify that:\"\n\n    1.  ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``.\n\n        Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n        ```math\n        0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x-a\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n        ```\n\n        Clearly, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``\n\n    2.  ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c`` (``c`` is a constant).\n\n        Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n        ```math\n        0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|c-c\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n        ```\n\n        Since ``c-c=0``, we can use any positive number for ``\\delta`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c``.\n\n    3.  ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\n        Here ``a=2`` and ``L=4``. Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n        ```math\n        0<\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x^2-4\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n        ```\n\n        Now,\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|x^2-4\\right|=\\left|\\left(x+2\\right)\\left(x-2\\right)\\right|=\\left|x+2\\right|\\left|x-2\\right|\\,.\n        ```\n\n        We want the expression above to be less than ``\\varepsilon``. We can make the factor ``\\left|x-2\\right|`` as small as we wish by choosing ``\\delta`` properly, but we need to control the factor ``\\left|x+2\\right|`` so that it does not become too large.\n\n        If we first assume ``\\delta\\le1`` and require that ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta``, then we have\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|x-2\\right|<1 \\implies 1<x<3 \\implies 3<x+2<5 \\implies \\left|x+2\\right|<5\n        ```\n\n        Hence,\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-4\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\le1\\,.\n        ```\n\n        But ``5\\left|x-2\\right|<\\varepsilon`` if ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``. Therefore, if we take ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace1,\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}\\right\\rbrace``, the *minimum* of the two numbers ``1`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``, then\n\n        ```math\n        \\left|f\\left(x-4\\right)\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|<5\\times\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}=\\varepsilon\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\,.\n        ```\n\n        This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\nWe do not usually rely on the formal definition of limit to verify specific limits such as those in the last example. Rather, we appeal to general theorems about limits in particular the theorems of the next section.\n\nIf a function has a limit at a point, this limit is *unique*.\n\n::: proposition\nIf ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, then ``L=M``.\n:::\n\n::: proof \"by contradiction\"\nSuppose ``L\\ne M``.\n\nLet ``\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}>0``. \n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, there exists ``\\delta_1 > 0`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\n```\n\nand since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, there exists ``\\delta_2>0`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nLet ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon``. Therefore,\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|L-M\\right|=&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\\\le&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\<&2\\varepsilon=2\\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}=\\left|L-M\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nby the triangle inequality.\n\nThis is a contradiction, so ``L=M``.\n:::\n\nAlthough a function ``f`` can only have one limit at any particular point, it is, nevertheless, useful to be able to describe the behaviour of functions that approach different numbers a ``x`` tends to ``a`` from one side or the other.","resultHTML":"<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)</span> is any strict positive number, <em>no matter how small</em>, we must be able to ensure that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)</span> by restricting <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> to be <em>close enough</em> to (but not equal to) <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>. How close is close enough? It is sufficient that the distance <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|\\)</span> from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> be less than a positive number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span> that depends on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)</span>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<h5>Formal Definition.</h5>\n<p>We say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f:X\\mapsto Y\\)</span> approaches the limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> tends to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, and we write</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>if the following condition is satisfied:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\forall\\varepsilon&gt;0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)&gt;0:0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note the possible dependency of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span> on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)</span> and the fact that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> belongs to the domain of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>The formal definition of limit does not tell you how to find the limit of a function, but it does enable you to verify that a suspected limit is correct.</p>\n<div data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Verify that:</h5>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)</span> be given. We must find <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)</span> so that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\implies\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Clearly, we can take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta=\\varepsilon\\)</span> and the implication above will be true. This proves that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a\\)</span></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c\\)</span> (<span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span> is a constant).</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)</span> be given. We must find <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)</span> so that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\implies\\left|c-c\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c-c=0\\)</span>, we can use any positive number for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span> and the implication above will be true. This proves that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c\\)</span>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a=2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L=4\\)</span>. Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)</span> be given. We must find <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)</span> so that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;\\delta\\implies\\left|x^2-4\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Now,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left|x^2-4\\right|=\\left|\\left(x+2\\right)\\left(x-2\\right)\\right|=\\left|x+2\\right|\\left|x-2\\right|\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>We want the expression above to be less than <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)</span>. We can make the factor <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-2\\right|\\)</span> as small as we wish by choosing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span> properly, but we need to control the factor <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x+2\\right|\\)</span> so that it does not become too large.</p>\n<p>If we first assume <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\le1\\)</span> and require that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;\\delta\\)</span>, then we have</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;1 \\implies 1&lt;x&lt;3 \\implies 3&lt;x+2&lt;5 \\implies \\left|x+2\\right|&lt;5\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Hence,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-4\\right|&lt;5\\left|x-2\\right|\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;\\delta\\le1\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>But <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(5\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)</span> if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}\\)</span>. Therefore, if we take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace1,\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}\\right\\rbrace\\)</span>, the <em>minimum</em> of the two numbers <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left|f\\left(x-4\\right)\\right|&lt;5\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;5\\times\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}=\\varepsilon\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|&lt;\\delta\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This proves that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4\\)</span>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<p>We do not usually rely on the formal definition of limit to verify specific limits such as those in the last example. Rather, we appeal to general theorems about limits in particular the theorems of the next section.</p>\n<p>If a function has a limit at a point, this limit is <em>unique</em>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"proposition\">\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L=M\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<h5>by contradiction</h5>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\ne M\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}&gt;0\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span>, there exists <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta_1 &gt; 0\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>and since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M\\)</span>, there exists <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta_2&gt;0\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_2\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_1\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_2\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)</span>. Therefore,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|L-M\\right|=&amp;\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\\\le&amp;\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\&lt;&amp;2\\varepsilon=2\\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}=\\left|L-M\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>by the triangle inequality.</p>\n<p>This is a contradiction, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L=M\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Although a function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> can only have one limit at any particular point, it is, nevertheless, useful to be able to describe the behaviour of functions that approach different numbers a <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> tends to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> from one side or the other.</p>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 255,\n    height: 155,\n    caption: \"\\\\(\\\\lim_{x\\\\to0^-}\\\\operatorname{sgn} x=-1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\lim_{x\\\\to0^+}\\\\operatorname{sgn} x=1\\\\).\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [125, 80],\n    scale: 50,\n    x: { ticks: [-2, -1, 1, 2] },\n    y: { ticks: [-1, 1] },\n});\n\nfigure.plot(() => -1, {\n    domain: [-3, 0],\n    samples: 2,\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\nfigure.plot(() => 1, {\n    domain: [0, 3],\n    samples: 2,\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\n\nfigure.point(0, -1, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"#ffffff\",\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\nfigure.point(0, 1, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"#ffffff\",\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", { x: 109, y: 80, latex: true, width: 14, height: 18 });\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy−2−112−11Olimx→0−⁡sgn⁡x=−1 and limx→0+⁡sgn⁡x=1.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"255\" height=\"155\" viewBox=\"0 0 255 155\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"255,80 248,76 248,84\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"125,0 121,7 129,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"241\" y=\"50\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"133\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"21\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"71\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"175\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"171\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"221\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"121\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"129\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"101\" y=\"118\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"121\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"129\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"101\" y=\"18\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><polyline points=\"-25,130 125,130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><polyline points=\"125,30 275,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"30\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"109\" y=\"80\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"::: definition\nWe say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *right limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n```\n\nif the following condition is satisfied:\n\n```math\n\\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a<x<a+\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nSimilarly, we say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *left limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n```\n\nif the following condition is satisfied:\n\n```math\n\\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a-\\delta<x<a\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n:::\n\nNote again the dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Show that ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\"\n\n    Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. If ``x>0``, then ``\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|=\\sqrt x``. We can ensure that ``\\sqrt x<\\varepsilon`` by requiring ``x<\\varepsilon^2``. Thus, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon^2`` and the condition of the definition will be satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    0<x<\\delta=\\varepsilon^2\\implies\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Therefore, ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\n\nThe existence of different right and left limits of a function at a point excludes the existence of a limit at that point.\n\n::: theorem\nA function ``f`` has limit ``L`` at ``x=a`` if and only if it has both left and right limits there and these one-sided limits are both equal to ``L``:\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\iff\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"What one-sided limits does ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}`` have at ``x=-1`` and ``x=1``?\"\n\n    The domain of ``g`` is ``\\left[-1,1\\right]``, so ``g`` is defined only to the right of ``x=-1`` and only to the left of ``x=1``.\n    \n    As can be seen in the figure,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to-1^+}g\\left(x\\right)=0\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\lim_{x\\to1^-}g\\left(x\\right)=0\n    ```\n\n    ``g`` has no left limit or limit at ``x=-1``, no right limit or limit at ``x=1``.","output":"","result":"::: definition\nWe say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *right limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n```\n\nif the following condition is satisfied:\n\n```math\n\\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a<x<a+\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nSimilarly, we say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *left limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n```\n\nif the following condition is satisfied:\n\n```math\n\\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a-\\delta<x<a\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n:::\n\nNote again the dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Show that ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\"\n\n    Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. If ``x>0``, then ``\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|=\\sqrt x``. We can ensure that ``\\sqrt x<\\varepsilon`` by requiring ``x<\\varepsilon^2``. Thus, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon^2`` and the condition of the definition will be satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    0<x<\\delta=\\varepsilon^2\\implies\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Therefore, ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\n\nThe existence of different right and left limits of a function at a point excludes the existence of a limit at that point.\n\n::: theorem\nA function ``f`` has limit ``L`` at ``x=a`` if and only if it has both left and right limits there and these one-sided limits are both equal to ``L``:\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\iff\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"What one-sided limits does ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}`` have at ``x=-1`` and ``x=1``?\"\n\n    The domain of ``g`` is ``\\left[-1,1\\right]``, so ``g`` is defined only to the right of ``x=-1`` and only to the left of ``x=1``.\n    \n    As can be seen in the figure,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to-1^+}g\\left(x\\right)=0\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\lim_{x\\to1^-}g\\left(x\\right)=0\n    ```\n\n    ``g`` has no left limit or limit at ``x=-1``, no right limit or limit at ``x=1``.","resultHTML":"<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<p>We say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f:X\\mapsto Y\\)</span> has <em>right limit</em> <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, and we write</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>if the following condition is satisfied:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\forall \\varepsilon&gt;0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)&gt;0:a&lt;x&lt;a+\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Similarly, we say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f:X\\mapsto Y\\)</span> has <em>left limit</em> <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, and we write</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>if the following condition is satisfied:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\forall \\varepsilon&gt;0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)&gt;0:a-\\delta&lt;x&lt;a\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note again the dependency of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span> on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)</span> and the fact that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> belongs to the domain of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span>.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0\\)</span>.</h5>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)</span> be given. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|=\\sqrt x\\)</span>. We can ensure that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sqrt x&lt;\\varepsilon\\)</span> by requiring <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x&lt;\\varepsilon^2\\)</span>. Thus, we can take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta=\\varepsilon^2\\)</span> and the condition of the definition will be satisfied:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;x&lt;\\delta=\\varepsilon^2\\implies\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Therefore, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0\\)</span>.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>The existence of different right and left limits of a function at a point excludes the existence of a limit at that point.</p>\n<div data-type=\"theorem\">\n<p>A function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=a\\)</span> if and only if it has both left and right limits there and these one-sided limits are both equal to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span>:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\iff\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>What one-sided limits does <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\)</span> have at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=-1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>?</h5>\n<p>The domain of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[-1,1\\right]\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> is defined only to the right of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=-1\\)</span> and only to the left of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>As can be seen in the figure,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to-1^+}g\\left(x\\right)=0\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\lim_{x\\to1^-}g\\left(x\\right)=0\n\\]\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> has no left limit or limit at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=-1\\)</span>, no right limit or limit at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>.</p>\n</aside>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 255,\n    height: 160,\n    caption: \"\\\\(\\\\sqrt{1 - x^2}\\\\) has right limit \\\\(0\\\\) at \\\\(-1\\\\) and left limit \\\\(0\\\\) at \\\\(1\\\\)\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [125, 130],\n    scale: 100,\n    x: { ticks: [-1, 1] },\n    y: { ticks: [1] },\n});\n\nfigure.plot(\n    (x) => Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, 1 - x ** 2)),\n    { domain: [-1, 1], samples: 201, stroke: \"#ff0000\" },\n);\n\nfor (const x of [-1, 1]) {\n    figure.point(x, 0, {\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: \"#ff0000\",\n        stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n    });\n}\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", { x: 109, y: 130, latex: true, width: 14, height: 18 });\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy−111O1−x2 has right limit 0 at −1 and left limit 0 at 1","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"255\" height=\"160\" viewBox=\"0 0 255 160\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"255,130 248,126 248,134\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"125,0 121,7 129,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"241\" y=\"100\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"133\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"134\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"21\" y=\"138\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"134\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"221\" y=\"138\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"121\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"129\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"101\" y=\"18\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><polyline points=\"25,130 26,115.8932640203341 27,110.10025125786758 28,105.68950843771356 29,102 30,98.775010008008 31,95.88255578153603 32,93.24404810102178 33,90.80816411546915 34,88.53917511674425 35,86.41101056459327 36,84.40394753928801 37,82.50263165184833 38,80.6948278575158 39,78.9705967113077 40,77.3217312357363 41,75.74136013499785 42,74.22366093046264 43,72.76364791498327 44,71.35701235441701 45,70.00000000000001 46,68.68931577612267 47,67.42204861135194 48,66.19561143620291 49,65.00769276291231 50,63.85621722338523 51,62.73931311679905 52,61.655285500632885 53,60.60259370841011 54,59.57983243416699 55,58.585715714571506 56,57.61906328320971 57,56.67878888070655 58,55.763890188130134 59.000000000000014,54.873440116028206 60,54.00657923214669 61,53.1625091508058 62,52.3404867385843 63,51.539819016267884 64,50.759858657369875 65,50 66,49.25967550226