95745643593003 160,36.32503002402403 161,36.7047696824752 162,37.09682459678787 163,37.501351361222575 164,37.91851434734558 165,38.34848610088319 166,38.79144776941145 167,39.24758956369257 168,39.717111255786676 169,40.200222717425405 170,40.697144502541235 171,41.20810847830754 172,41.733358509570564 173,42.273151202154764 174,42.82775671120996 175,43.39745962155614 176,43.982559907888444 177,44.58337398374951 178,45.20023584938458 179,45.83349834999676 180,46.483534557549675 181,47.150739291168094 182,47.83553079341412 183,48.538352582334795 184,49.2596755022622 185,50 186,50.759858657369875 187,51.539819016267884 188,52.3404867385843 189,53.1625091508058 190,54.00657923214668 191,54.873440116028206 192,55.763890188130134 193,56.67878888070655 194,57.61906328320971 195,58.585715714571506 196,59.57983243416699 197,60.60259370841011 198,61.655285500632885 199,62.73931311679905 200,63.85621722338523 201,65.00769276291231 202,66.19561143620291 203,67.42204861135194 204,68.68931577612267 205,70.00000000000001 206,71.35701235441701 207,72.76364791498327 208,74.22366093046264 209,75.74136013499788 210,77.32173123573632 211,78.97059671130772 212,80.69482785751583 213,82.50263165184832 214,84.403947539288 215,86.41101056459325 216,88.53917511674422 217,90.80816411546913 218,93.24404810102178 219,95.88255578153603 220,98.775010008008 221,102 222,105.68950843771356 223,110.10025125786758 224,115.8932640203341 225,130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"25\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><circle cx=\"225\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"109\" y=\"130\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption><math><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></math> has right limit <math><mn>0</mn></math> at <math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and left limit <math><mn>0</mn></math> at <math><mn>1</mn></math></figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"## Rules and Theorems about Limits\n\nThe following rules make it easy to calculate limits and one-sided limits of many kinds of functions when we know some elementary limits.\n\n::: proposition \"Limit Rules\"\nIf ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``k`` is a constant, then\n\n1. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M`` (limit of a sum)\n2. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L-M`` (limit of a difference)\n3. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=LM`` (limit of a product)\n4. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}kf\\left(x\\right)=kL`` (limit of a multiple)\n5. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f\\left(x\\right)}{g\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{L}{M}\\textrm{ if }M\\ne0`` (limit of quotient)\n6. If ``m\\in \\mathbb{Z}`` and ``n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0``, then ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)\\right)^\\frac{m}{n}=L^\\frac{m}{n}``, provided ``L>0`` if ``n`` is even and ``L\\ne0`` if ``m <0``. (limit of a power)\n7. If ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an interval containing ``a`` in its interior, then ``L\\le M``. (order is preserved)\n:::\n\nRules 1—6 are also valid for one-sided limits. So is rule 7, under the assumption that ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an open interval extending from ``a`` in the appropriate direction.\n\n::: proof \"limit of a sum\"\nLet ``\\varepsilon > 0`` be given.\n\nWe want to find a strict positive number ``\\delta`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon\n```\n\nObserve that\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|=&\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right)+\\left(g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right)\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nby the triangle inequality.\n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_1`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n```\n\nSimilarly, since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_2`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n```\n\nLet ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``. Therefore,\n\n```math\n\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nThis shows that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Find ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}``.\"\n\n    The expression ``\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}`` is formed by combining the basic functions ``x`` and ``c`` (constant) using addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. The previous theorem assures us that the limit of such a combination is the same combination of the limits ``a`` and ``c`` of the basic functions, provided the denominator does not have limit zero. Thus,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}=\\frac{a^2+a+4}{a^3-2a^2+7}\\quad\\textrm{provided }a^3-2a^2+7\\ne0\n    ```\n\nThe result of the example can be generalized as a direct corollary.\n\n::: corollary\nIf ``a\\in \\mathbb{R}`` and\n\n1.  ``P\\left(x\\right)`` is a polynomial, then\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}P\\left(x\\right)=P\\left(a\\right)\n    ```\n\n2.  ``P\\left(x\\right)`` and ``Q\\left(x\\right)`` are polynomials and ``Q\\left(a\\right)\\ne 0``, then\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{P\\left(a\\right)}{Q\\left(a\\right)}\\,.\n    ```\n:::\n\nThe following theorem will enable us to calculate some very important limits in subsequent chapters. It is called the *squeeze theorem* because it refers to a function ``g`` whose values are squeezed between the values of two other functions that have the same limit ``L`` at a point ``a``. Being trapped between the values of two functions that approach ``L``, the values of ``g`` must also approach ``L``.","output":"","result":"## Rules and Theorems about Limits\n\nThe following rules make it easy to calculate limits and one-sided limits of many kinds of functions when we know some elementary limits.\n\n::: proposition \"Limit Rules\"\nIf ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``k`` is a constant, then\n\n1. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M`` (limit of a sum)\n2. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L-M`` (limit of a difference)\n3. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=LM`` (limit of a product)\n4. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}kf\\left(x\\right)=kL`` (limit of a multiple)\n5. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f\\left(x\\right)}{g\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{L}{M}\\textrm{ if }M\\ne0`` (limit of quotient)\n6. If ``m\\in \\mathbb{Z}`` and ``n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0``, then ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)\\right)^\\frac{m}{n}=L^\\frac{m}{n}``, provided ``L>0`` if ``n`` is even and ``L\\ne0`` if ``m <0``. (limit of a power)\n7. If ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an interval containing ``a`` in its interior, then ``L\\le M``. (order is preserved)\n:::\n\nRules 1—6 are also valid for one-sided limits. So is rule 7, under the assumption that ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an open interval extending from ``a`` in the appropriate direction.\n\n::: proof \"limit of a sum\"\nLet ``\\varepsilon > 0`` be given.\n\nWe want to find a strict positive number ``\\delta`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon\n```\n\nObserve that\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|=&\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right)+\\left(g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right)\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nby the triangle inequality.\n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_1`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n```\n\nSimilarly, since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_2`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n```\n\nLet ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``. Therefore,\n\n```math\n\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nThis shows that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Find ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}``.\"\n\n    The expression ``\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}`` is formed by combining the basic functions ``x`` and ``c`` (constant) using addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. The previous theorem assures us that the limit of such a combination is the same combination of the limits ``a`` and ``c`` of the basic functions, provided the denominator does not have limit zero. Thus,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}=\\frac{a^2+a+4}{a^3-2a^2+7}\\quad\\textrm{provided }a^3-2a^2+7\\ne0\n    ```\n\nThe result of the example can be generalized as a direct corollary.\n\n::: corollary\nIf ``a\\in \\mathbb{R}`` and\n\n1.  ``P\\left(x\\right)`` is a polynomial, then\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}P\\left(x\\right)=P\\left(a\\right)\n    ```\n\n2.  ``P\\left(x\\right)`` and ``Q\\left(x\\right)`` are polynomials and ``Q\\left(a\\right)\\ne 0``, then\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{P\\left(a\\right)}{Q\\left(a\\right)}\\,.\n    ```\n:::\n\nThe following theorem will enable us to calculate some very important limits in subsequent chapters. It is called the *squeeze theorem* because it refers to a function ``g`` whose values are squeezed between the values of two other functions that have the same limit ``L`` at a point ``a``. Being trapped between the values of two functions that approach ``L``, the values of ``g`` must also approach ``L``.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2649-rules-and-theorems-about-limits\">\n<h1>Rules and Theorems about Limits</h1>\n<p>The following rules make it easy to calculate limits and one-sided limits of many kinds of functions when we know some elementary limits.</p>\n<div data-type=\"proposition\">\n<h5>Limit Rules</h5>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(k\\)</span> is a constant, then</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M\\)</span> (limit of a sum)</li>\n<li><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L-M\\)</span> (limit of a difference)</li>\n<li><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=LM\\)</span> (limit of a product)</li>\n<li><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}kf\\left(x\\right)=kL\\)</span> (limit of a multiple)</li>\n<li><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f\\left(x\\right)}{g\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{L}{M}\\textrm{ if }M\\ne0\\)</span> (limit of quotient)</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)\\right)^\\frac{m}{n}=L^\\frac{m}{n}\\)</span>, provided <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L&gt;0\\)</span> if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> is even and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\ne0\\)</span> if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m &lt;0\\)</span>. (limit of a power)</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> on an interval containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> in its interior, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\le M\\)</span>. (order is preserved)</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<p>Rules 1—6 are also valid for one-sided limits. So is rule 7, under the assumption that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> on an open interval extending from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> in the appropriate direction.</p>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<h5>limit of a sum</h5>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon &gt; 0\\)</span> be given.</p>\n<p>We want to find a strict positive number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\implies\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Observe that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|=&amp;\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right)+\\left(g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right)\\right|\\\\\n\\le&amp;\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>by the triangle inequality.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\)</span> is a strict positive number, there exists a number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta_1\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Similarly, since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\)</span> is a strict positive number, there exists a number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta_2\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_2\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_1\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_2\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\)</span>. Therefore,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This shows that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Find <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}\\)</span>.</h5>\n<p>The expression <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}\\)</span> is formed by combining the basic functions <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span> (constant) using addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. The previous theorem assures us that the limit of such a combination is the same combination of the limits <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span> of the basic functions, provided the denominator does not have limit zero. Thus,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}=\\frac{a^2+a+4}{a^3-2a^2+7}\\quad\\textrm{provided }a^3-2a^2+7\\ne0\n\\]\n</div>\n</aside>\n<p>The result of the example can be generalized as a direct corollary.</p>\n<div data-type=\"corollary\">\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\in \\mathbb{R}\\)</span> and</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> is a polynomial, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a}P\\left(x\\right)=P\\left(a\\right)\n\\]\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> are polynomials and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q\\left(a\\right)\\ne 0\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{P\\left(a\\right)}{Q\\left(a\\right)}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<p>The following theorem will enable us to calculate some very important limits in subsequent chapters. It is called the <em>squeeze theorem</em> because it refers to a function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> whose values are squeezed between the values of two other functions that have the same limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> at a point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>. Being trapped between the values of two functions that approach <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span>, the values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> must also approach <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 40;\nconst red = \"#ff0000\";\nconst green = \"#008000\";\nconst blue = \"#4169e1\";\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 6 * scale,\n    height: 4.5 * scale+10,\n    caption: \"The graph of g is squeezed between those of f and h.\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [scale, 4 * scale],\n    scale,\n    x: { ticks: [{ value: 3, label: \"a\", length: 1 }] },\n    y: { ticks: [{ value: 2, label: \"L\", length: 1 }] },\n});\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(4 * scale).y1(4 * scale)\n    .x2(4 * scale).y2(2 * scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(scale).y1(2 * scale)\n    .x2(4 * scale).y2(2 * scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nconst plotOptions = { domain: [-1, 5], samples: 601 };\n\nfigure.plot((x) => 2 + 0.3 * (x - 3) ** 2, {\n    ...plotOptions,\n    stroke: green,\n});\nfigure.plot((x) => 1 + Math.cos(x - 3), {\n    ...plotOptions,\n    stroke: blue,\n});\nfigure.plot((x) => 2 - 0.1 * (x - 3) ** 2, {\n    ...plotOptions,\n    stroke: red,\n});\n\nfigure.point(3, 2, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"#ffffff\",\n    stroke: red,\n});\n\nfigure.label(\"\\\\color[HTML]{#ff0000}y = g(x)\", {\n    x: scale,\n    y: 1.9 * scale,\n    latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"\\\\color[HTML]{#008000}y = h(x)\", {\n    x: scale,\n    y: 1.2 * scale,\n    latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"\\\\color[HTML]{#4169e1}y = f(x)\", {\n    x: scale,\n    y: 2.6 * scale,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xyaLy=g(x)y=h(x)y=f(x)The graph of g is squeezed between those of f and h.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"240\" height=\"190\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 190\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"240,160 233,156 233,164\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"40,0 36,7 44,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"226\" y=\"130\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"48\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"164\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"153\" y=\"168\" width=\"14\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"26\" y=\"68\" width=\"14\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><polyline points=\"0,-112 0.3999999999999986,-111.0412 0.7999999999999972,-110.08479999999997 1.2000000000000028,-109.13079999999997 1.6000000000000014,-108.17919999999998 2,-107.23000000000002 2.4000000000000057,-106.28319999999997 2.8000000000000043,-105.33879999999999 3.1999999999999957,-104.39679999999998 3.6000000000000014,-103.4572 4,-102.51999999999998 4.399999999999999,-101.58519999999999 4.799999999999997,-100.65279999999996 5.200000000000003,-99.7228 5.600000000000001,-98.79520000000002 6,-97.87 6.399999999999999,-96.94720000000001 6.800000000000004,-96.02679999999998 7.199999999999996,-95.1088 7.599999999999994,-94.19319999999999 8,-93.28 8.399999999999999,-92.3692 8.799999999999997,-91.4608 9.2,-90.5548 9.600000000000001,-89.65119999999999 10,-88.75 10.399999999999999,-87.85120000000003 10.8,-86.9548 11.200000000000003,-86.06079999999997 11.600000000000001,-85.16919999999999 12,-84.28 12.400