0002,80.2304 170,80.25 170.39999999999998,80.2704 170.79999999999998,80.2916 171.20000000000002,80.31360000000001 171.6,80.3364 172,80.36 172.39999999999998,80.3844 172.8,80.4096 173.2,80.43560000000001 173.6,80.4624 174,80.49 174.4,80.5184 174.8,80.5476 175.2,80.5776 175.6,80.6084 176,80.64 176.4,80.6724 176.8,80.7056 177.2,80.7396 177.60000000000002,80.7744 178,80.81 178.4,80.8464 178.79999999999998,80.8836 179.20000000000002,80.9216 179.60000000000002,80.9604 180,81 180.39999999999998,81.04039999999999 180.79999999999998,81.0816 181.20000000000002,81.1236 181.6,81.16640000000001 182,81.21 182.39999999999998,81.2544 182.8,81.2996 183.2,81.3456 183.6,81.3924 184,81.44 184.4,81.4884 184.8,81.5376 185.2,81.5876 185.6,81.63839999999999 186,81.69 186.4,81.7424 186.8,81.79560000000001 187.2,81.84960000000001 187.60000000000002,81.90440000000001 188,81.96 188.4,82.0164 188.79999999999998,82.0736 189.20000000000002,82.1316 189.60000000000002,82.1904 190,82.25 190.39999999999998,82.3104 190.79999999999998,82.3716 191.20000000000002,82.4336 191.6,82.49640000000001 192,82.56 192.39999999999998,82.6244 192.8,82.6896 193.2,82.7556 193.6,82.8224 194,82.89 194.4,82.9584 194.8,83.0276 195.2,83.0976 195.6,83.1684 196,83.24 196.4,83.3124 196.8,83.3856 197.2,83.4596 197.60000000000002,83.5344 198,83.61 198.4,83.6864 198.79999999999998,83.7636 199.20000000000002,83.8416 199.60000000000002,83.9204 200,84 200.39999999999998,84.0804 200.79999999999998,84.16159999999999 201.20000000000002,84.2436 201.6,84.3264 202,84.41 202.39999999999998,84.4944 202.8,84.5796 203.2,84.6656 203.6,84.7524 204,84.84 204.4,84.9284 204.8,85.0176 205.2,85.10759999999999 205.6,85.19839999999999 206,85.28999999999999 206.4,85.3824 206.8,85.4756 207.2,85.56960000000001 207.60000000000002,85.6644 208,85.76 208.4,85.8564 208.79999999999998,85.9536 209.20000000000002,86.05160000000001 209.60000000000002,86.1504 210,86.25 210.39999999999998,86.35040000000001 210.79999999999998,86.4516 211.20000000000002,86.5536 211.6,86.65639999999999 212,86.76 212.39999999999998,86.86439999999999 212.8,86.96960000000001 213.2,87.0756 213.6,87.1824 214,87.28999999999999 214.4,87.3984 214.8,87.5076 215.2,87.6176 215.6,87.7284 216,87.84 216.4,87.9524 216.8,88.0656 217.2,88.1796 217.60000000000002,88.29440000000001 218,88.41 218.4,88.5264 218.79999999999998,88.64359999999999 219.20000000000002,88.7616 219.60000000000002,88.88040000000001 220,89 220.39999999999998,89.1204 220.79999999999998,89.24159999999999 221.20000000000002,89.3636 221.6,89.4864 222,89.61 222.39999999999998,89.7344 222.8,89.8596 223.2,89.9856 223.6,90.1124 224,90.24 224.4,90.36840000000001 224.8,90.4976 225.2,90.6276 225.6,90.7584 226,90.89 226.4,91.0224 226.8,91.1556 227.2,91.2896 227.60000000000002,91.4244 228,91.56 228.4,91.6964 228.79999999999998,91.83359999999999 229.20000000000002,91.9716 229.60000000000002,92.1104 230,92.25 230.39999999999998,92.3904 230.79999999999998,92.5316 231.20000000000002,92.6736 231.6,92.8164 232,92.96 232.39999999999998,93.1044 232.8,93.24960000000002 233.2,93.3956 233.6,93.5424 234,93.69 234.4,93.83840000000001 234.8,93.9876 235.2,94.13759999999999 235.6,94.2884 236,94.44000000000001 236.4,94.5924 236.8,94.7456 237.2,94.89959999999999 237.60000000000002,95.05440000000002 238,95.21000000000001 238.4,95.3664 238.79999999999998,95.5236 239.20000000000002,95.6816 239.60000000000002,95.8404 240,96\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"80\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"40\" y=\"76\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi style=\"color:#ff0000;\">y</mi><mo style=\"color:#ff0000;\">=</mo><mi style=\"color:#ff0000;\">g</mi><mrow style=\"color:#ff0000;\"><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"40\" y=\"48\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi style=\"color:#008000;\">y</mi><mo style=\"color:#008000;\">=</mo><mi style=\"color:#008000;\">h</mi><mrow style=\"color:#008000;\"><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"40\" y=\"104\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi style=\"color:#4169e1;\">y</mi><mo style=\"color:#4169e1;\">=</mo><mi style=\"color:#4169e1;\">f</mi><mrow style=\"color:#4169e1;\"><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption>The graph of g is squeezed between those of f and h.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"::: theorem \"Squeeze theorem\"\nIf ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)\\le h\\left(x\\right)`` holds for all ``x`` in some open interval containing ``a``, except possibly at ``x=a``, and\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a}h\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n    ```\n    then,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n    ```\n:::\n\nSimilar statements hold for one-sided limits.\n\n::: proof\nLet ``\\varepsilon > 0`` be given.\n\nWe want to find a strict positive number ``\\delta`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\n```\n\nObserve that\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|=&\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|=\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L+L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\\\\\n```\n\nby the triangle inequality and the squeezing of ``g`` between ``f`` and ``h``.\n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_1`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\,.\n```\n\nSimilarly, since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}h\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_2`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\,.\n```\n\nLet ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}``. Therefore,\n\n```math\n\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\le\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nThis shows that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=L``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Show that if ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|=0``, then ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=0``.\"\n\n    Since ``-\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\le f\\left(x\\right)\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|``, and ``-\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|`` and ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|`` both have limit ``0`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, so does ``f\\left(x\\right)`` by the squeeze theorem.\n\n","output":"","result":"::: theorem \"Squeeze theorem\"\nIf ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)\\le h\\left(x\\right)`` holds for all ``x`` in some open interval containing ``a``, except possibly at ``x=a``, and\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a}h\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n    ```\n    then,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n    ```\n:::\n\nSimilar statements hold for one-sided limits.\n\n::: proof\nLet ``\\varepsilon > 0`` be given.\n\nWe want to find a strict positive number ``\\delta`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\n```\n\nObserve that\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|=&\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|=\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L+L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\\\\\n```\n\nby the triangle inequality and the squeezing of ``g`` between ``f`` and ``h``.\n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_1`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\,.\n```\n\nSimilarly, since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}h\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_2`` such that\n\n```math\n0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\,.\n```\n\nLet ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}``. Therefore,\n\n```math\n\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\le\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nThis shows that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=L``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Show that if ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|=0``, then ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=0``.\"\n\n    Since ``-\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\le f\\left(x\\right)\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|``, and ``-\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|`` and ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|`` both have limit ``0`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, so does ``f\\left(x\\right)`` by the squeeze theorem.\n\n","resultHTML":"<div data-type=\"theorem\">\n<h5>Squeeze theorem</h5>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)\\le h\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> holds for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> in some open interval containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, except possibly at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=a\\)</span>, and</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a}h\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>then,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Similar statements hold for one-sided limits.</p>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon &gt; 0\\)</span> be given.</p>\n<p>We want to find a strict positive number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Observe that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|=&amp;\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&amp;\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&amp;\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|=\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L+L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\\\\n\\le&amp;\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\n\\end{aligned}\\\\\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>by the triangle inequality and the squeezing of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> between <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\)</span> is a strict positive number, there exists a number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta_1\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Similarly, since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}h\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\)</span> is a strict positive number, there exists a number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta_2\\)</span> such that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_2\\implies\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0&lt;\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_1\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-a\\right|&lt;\\delta_2\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\)</span>. Therefore,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|\\le\\left|h\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This shows that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Show that if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=0\\)</span>.</h5>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\le f\\left(x\\right)\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\)</span> both have limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> tends to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>, so does <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> by the squeeze theorem.</p>\n</aside>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Limits at Infinity and Infinite Limits\n\nWe will extend the concept of limit to allow for two situations not covered by the definitions of limit and one-sided limit in the previous section:\n\n1. *limits at infinity*, where ``x`` becomes arbitrarily large, positive or negative;\n2. *infinite limits*, which are not real limits at all but provide useful symbolism for describing the behaviour of functions whose values become arbitrarily large, positive or negative.","output":"","result":"## Limits at Infinity and Infinite Limits\n\nWe will extend the concept of limit to allow for two situations not covered by the definitions of limit and one-sided limit in the previous section:\n\n1. *limits at infinity*, where ``x`` becomes arbitrarily large, positive or negative;\n2. *infinite limits*, which are not real limits at all but provide useful symbolism for describing the behaviour of functions whose values become arbitrarily large, positive or negative.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2652-limits-at-infinity-and-infinite-limits\">\n<h1>Limits at Infinity and Infinite Limits</h1>\n<p>We will extend the concept of limit to allow for two situations not covered by the definitions of limit and one-sided limit in the previous section:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>limits at infinity</em>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> becomes arbitrarily large, positive or negative;</li>\n<li><em>infinite limits</em>, which are not real limits at all but provide useful symbolism for describing the behaviour of functions whose values become arbitrarily large, positive or negative.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const g = (x) => x / Math.sqrt(x ** 2 + 1);\nconst pairs = [[-1000, 1], [-100, 10], [-10, 100], [1, 1000]];\nconst formula = String.raw`f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}`;\nconst rounded = (x) => String(Number(g(x).toFixed(9)));\n\nJSCells.table({\n    caption: \"What happens with \\\\(\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{x² + 1}}\\\\) when \\\\(x\\\\) approaches \\\\(\\\\pm\\\\infty\\\\).\",\n    headers: [\n        JSCells.latex(\"x\"),\n        JSCells.latex(formula),\n        JSCells.latex(\"x\"),\n        JSCells.latex(formula),\n    ],\n    rows: pairs.map(([x1, x2]) => [\n        JSCells.latex(String(x1)),\n        JSCells.latex(rounded(x1)),\n        JSCells.latex(String(x2)),\n        JSCells.latex(rounded(x2)),\n    ]),\n    align: [\"center\", \"center\", \"center\", \"center\"],\n});","output":"","result":"What happens with xx2+1 when x approaches ±∞.xf(x)=xx2+1xf(x)=xx2+1−1000−0.999999510.707106781−100−0.999950004100.99503719−10−0.995037191000.99995000410.70710678110000.9999995","resultHTML":"<figure data-type=\"table\"><figcaption>What happens with <math><mfrac><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></math> when <math><mi>x</mi></math> approaches <math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">±</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table><thead><tr><th class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span></th><th class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></math></span></th><th class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span></th><th class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></math></span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>0.9999995</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.707106781</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>0.999950004</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>10</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.99503719</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>0.99503719</mn></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>100</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.999950004</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.707106781</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1000</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.9999995</mn></math></span></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"const scale = 40;\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 12 * scale,\n    height: 3.5 * scale,\n    caption: \"The graph of \\\\(\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{x² + 1}}\\\\).\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [6 * scale, 2 * scale],\n    scale,\n    y: {\n        ticks: [\n            { value: 1, length: 1 },\n            { value: -1, length: 2 },\n        ],\n    },\n});\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(6 * scale).y1(scale)\n    .x2(12 * scale).y2(scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(0).y1(3 * scale)\n    .x2(6 * scale).y2(3 * scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.plot(\n    (x) => x / Math.sqrt(x ** 2 + 1),\n    { domain: [-6, 6], samples: 1201, stroke: \"#ff0000\" },\n);\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", {\n    x: 6 * scale - 16,\n    y: 2 * scale,\n    latex: true,\n    width: 14,\n    height: 18,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy1−1OThe graph of xx2+1.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"480\" height=\"140\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 140\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"480\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"480,80 473,76 473,84\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"240,0 236,7 244,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"466\" y=\"50\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"248\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"236\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"244\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"226\" y=\"28\" width=\"14\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"236\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"244\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"212\" y=\"108\" width=\"28\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"480\" y2=\"40