\left(x\\right)`` become larger and larger positive numbers, and we say that ``f`` has right-hand limit infinity at ``x=0``:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to0^+}f\\left(x\\right)=\\infty\n    ```\n\n    Similarly, the values of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` become larger and larger negative numbers as ``x`` approaches ``0`` from the left, so ``f`` has left-hand limit ``-\\infty`` at ``x=0``:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to0^-}f\\left(x\\right)=-\\infty\n    ```\n\n    These statements do not say that the one-sided limits *exist*; they do not exist because ``\\infty`` and ``-\\infty`` are not numbers. Since the one-sided limits are not equal even as infinite symbols, all we can say about the two-sided ``\\lim_{x\\to0}f\\left(x\\right)`` is that it does not exist.\n\nWe can now say a bit more about the limits at infinity and negative infinity of a rational function whose numerator has higher degree than the denominator. Earlier we said that such a limit *does not exist*. This is true, but we can assign ``\\infty`` or ``-\\infty`` to such limits, as the following example shows.\n\n!!! example \"Evaluate \"\n    \n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x^3+1}{x^2+1}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Divide the numerator and the denominator by ``x^2``, the largest power of the denominator:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x^3+1}{x^2+1}=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x+\\frac{1}{x^2}}{1+\\frac{1}{x^2}}=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}x+\\frac{1}{x^2}}{1}=\\infty\\,.\n    ```\n\nA polynomial ``Q\\left(x\\right)`` of degree ``n>0`` can have at most ``n`` *zeros*; that is, there are at most ``n`` different real numbers ``r`` for which ``Q\\left(r\\right)=0``. If ``Q\\left(r\\right)`` is the denominator of a rational function ``\\displaystyle R\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}``, that function will be defined for all ``x`` except those finitely many zeros of ``Q``. At each of those zeros, ``R\\left(x\\right)`` may have limits, infinite limits, or one-sided infinite limits. Here are some examples.\n\n!!! example\n\n    1. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\left(x- 2\\right)^2}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\left(x- 2\\right)^2}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{x+2}=0``.\n\n    2. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 2}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 2}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{1}{4}``.\n\n    3. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=-\\infty``.\n\n    4. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\infty``.\n\n    5. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}`` does not exist.\n\n    6. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-x}{\\left(x-2\\right)^3}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{-\\left(x-2\\right)}{\\left(x-2\\right)^3}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{-1}{\\left(x-2\\right)^2}=-\\infty``.\n\n","output":"","result":"!!! example\n\n    As ``x`` approaches ``0`` from the right, the values of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` become larger and larger positive numbers, and we say that ``f`` has right-hand limit infinity at ``x=0``:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to0^+}f\\left(x\\right)=\\infty\n    ```\n\n    Similarly, the values of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` become larger and larger negative numbers as ``x`` approaches ``0`` from the left, so ``f`` has left-hand limit ``-\\infty`` at ``x=0``:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to0^-}f\\left(x\\right)=-\\infty\n    ```\n\n    These statements do not say that the one-sided limits *exist*; they do not exist because ``\\infty`` and ``-\\infty`` are not numbers. Since the one-sided limits are not equal even as infinite symbols, all we can say about the two-sided ``\\lim_{x\\to0}f\\left(x\\right)`` is that it does not exist.\n\nWe can now say a bit more about the limits at infinity and negative infinity of a rational function whose numerator has higher degree than the denominator. Earlier we said that such a limit *does not exist*. This is true, but we can assign ``\\infty`` or ``-\\infty`` to such limits, as the following example shows.\n\n!!! example \"Evaluate \"\n    \n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x^3+1}{x^2+1}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Divide the numerator and the denominator by ``x^2``, the largest power of the denominator:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x^3+1}{x^2+1}=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x+\\frac{1}{x^2}}{1+\\frac{1}{x^2}}=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}x+\\frac{1}{x^2}}{1}=\\infty\\,.\n    ```\n\nA polynomial ``Q\\left(x\\right)`` of degree ``n>0`` can have at most ``n`` *zeros*; that is, there are at most ``n`` different real numbers ``r`` for which ``Q\\left(r\\right)=0``. If ``Q\\left(r\\right)`` is the denominator of a rational function ``\\displaystyle R\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}``, that function will be defined for all ``x`` except those finitely many zeros of ``Q``. At each of those zeros, ``R\\left(x\\right)`` may have limits, infinite limits, or one-sided infinite limits. Here are some examples.\n\n!!! example\n\n    1. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\left(x- 2\\right)^2}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\left(x- 2\\right)^2}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{x+2}=0``.\n\n    2. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 2}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 2}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{1}{4}``.\n\n    3. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=-\\infty``.\n\n    4. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\infty``.\n\n    5. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}`` does not exist.\n\n    6. ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-x}{\\left(x-2\\right)^3}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{-\\left(x-2\\right)}{\\left(x-2\\right)^3}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{-1}{\\left(x-2\\right)^2}=-\\infty``.\n\n","resultHTML":"<div data-type=\"example\">\n<p>As <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> approaches <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> from the right, the values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> become larger and larger positive numbers, and we say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has right-hand limit infinity at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=0\\)</span>:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f\\left(x\\right)=\\infty\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Similarly, the values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> become larger and larger negative numbers as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> approaches <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> from the left, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has left-hand limit <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-\\infty\\)</span> at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=0\\)</span>:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}f\\left(x\\right)=-\\infty\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>These statements do not say that the one-sided limits <em>exist</em>; they do not exist because <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\infty\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-\\infty\\)</span> are not numbers. Since the one-sided limits are not equal even as infinite symbols, all we can say about the two-sided <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to0}f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> is that it does not exist.</p>\n</div>\n<p>We can now say a bit more about the limits at infinity and negative infinity of a rational function whose numerator has higher degree than the denominator. Earlier we said that such a limit <em>does not exist</em>. This is true, but we can assign <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\infty\\)</span> or <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-\\infty\\)</span> to such limits, as the following example shows.</p>\n<div data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Evaluate </h5>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x^3+1}{x^2+1}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Divide the numerator and the denominator by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x^2\\)</span>, the largest power of the denominator:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x^3+1}{x^2+1}=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x+\\frac{1}{x^2}}{1+\\frac{1}{x^2}}=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}x+\\frac{1}{x^2}}{1}=\\infty\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>A polynomial <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> of degree <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n&gt;0\\)</span> can have at most <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> <em>zeros</em>; that is, there are at most <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> different real numbers <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> for which <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q\\left(r\\right)=0\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q\\left(r\\right)\\)</span> is the denominator of a rational function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle R\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}\\)</span>, that function will be defined for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> except those finitely many zeros of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q\\)</span>. At each of those zeros, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> may have limits, infinite limits, or one-sided infinite limits. Here are some examples.</p>\n<div data-type=\"example\">\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\left(x- 2\\right)^2}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\left(x- 2\\right)^2}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{x+2}=0\\)</span>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 2}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 2}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\)</span>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=-\\infty\\)</span>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}=\\infty\\)</span>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 3}{x^2-4}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x- 3}{\\left(x-2\\right)\\left(x+2\\right)}\\)</span> does not exist.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-x}{\\left(x-2\\right)^3}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{-\\left(x-2\\right)}{\\left(x-2\\right)^3}=\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{-1}{\\left(x-2\\right)^2}=-\\infty\\)</span>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Continuity Defined\n\nWhen a car is driven along a highway, its distance from its starting point depends on time in a *continuous* way, changing by small amounts over short intervals of time. But not all quantities change in this way. When the car is parked in a parking lot where the rate is quoted as \"€2,00 per hour or portion,\" the parking charges remain at €2,00 for the first hour and then suddenly jump to €4,00 as soon as the first hour has passed. The function relating parking charges to parking time will be called *discontinuous* at each hour.\n\nMost functions that we encounter have domains that are intervals, or unions of separate intervals. A point ``P`` in the domain of such a function is called an *interior point* of the domain if it belongs to some open interval contained in the domain. If it is not an interior point, then ``P`` is called an *endpoint* of the domain. For example, the domain of the function ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{4-x^2}`` is the closed interval ``\\left[-2,2\\right]``, which consists of interior points in the interval ``\\left]-2,2\\right[``, a left endpoint ``-2``, and a right endpoint ``2``. The domain of the function ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}`` is the union of open intervals ``\\left]-\\infty, 0\\right[\\cup\\left]0,\\infty\\right[`` and consists entirely of interior points. Note that although ``0`` is an endpoint of each of those intervals, it does not belong to the domain of ``g`` and so is not an endpoint of that domain.\n\n::: definition \"Continuity of a function at a point\"\n\nA function ``f``, defined on an open interval containing the point ``c``, an interior point, is said to be continuous at the point ``c`` if\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)\\,;\n```\n\nthat is,\n\n```math\n\\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nIf either ``\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)`` fails to exist or it exists but is not equal to ``f\\left(c\\right)``, then we will say that ``f`` is discontinuous at ``c``.\n:::\n\nIn graphical terms, ``f`` is continuous at an interior point ``c`` of its domain if its graph has no break in it at the point ``\\left(c,f\\left(x\\right)\\right)``; in other words, if you can draw the graph through that point without lifting your pen from the paper.","output":"","result":"## Continuity Defined\n\nWhen a car is driven along a highway, its distance from its starting point depends on time in a *continuous* way, changing by small amounts over short intervals of time. But not all quantities change in this way. When the car is parked in a parking lot where the rate is quoted as \"€2,00 per hour or portion,\" the parking charges remain at €2,00 for the first hour and then suddenly jump to €4,00 as soon as the first hour has passed. The function relating parking charges to parking time will be called *discontinuous* at each hour.\n\nMost functions that we encounter have domains that are intervals, or unions of separate intervals. A point ``P`` in the domain of such a function is called an *interior point* of the domain if it belongs to some open interval contained in the domain. If it is not an interior point, then ``P`` is called an *endpoint* of the domain. For example, the domain of the function ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{4-x^2}`` is the closed interval ``\\left[-2,2\\right]``, which consists of interior points in the interval ``\\left]-2,2\\right[``, a left endpoint ``-2``, and a right endpoint ``2``. The domain of the function ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}`` is the union of open intervals ``\\left]-\\infty, 0\\right[\\cup\\left]0,\\infty\\right[`` and consists entirely of interior points. Note that although ``0`` is an endpoint of each of those intervals, it does not belong to the domain of ``g`` and so is not an endpoint of that domain.\n\n::: definition \"Continuity of a function at a point\"\n\nA function ``f``, defined on an open interval containing the point ``c``, an interior point, is said to be continuous at the point ``c`` if\n\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)\\,;\n```\n\nthat is,\n\n```math\n\\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n```\n\nIf either ``\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)`` fails to exist or it exists but is not equal to ``f\\left(c\\right)``, then we will say that ``f`` is discontinuous at ``c``.\n:::\n\nIn graphical terms, ``f`` is continuous at an interior point ``c`` of its domain if its graph has no break in it at the point ``\\left(c,f\\left(x\\right)\\right)``; in other words, if you can draw the graph through that point without lifting your pen from the paper.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2660-continuity-defined\">\n<h1>Continuity Defined</h1>\n<p>When a car is driven along a highway, its distance from its starting point depends on time in a <em>continuous</em> way, changing by small amounts over short intervals of time. But not all quantities change in this way. When the car is parked in a parking lot where the rate is quoted as “€2,00 per hour or portion,” the parking charges remain at €2,00 for the first hour and then suddenly jump to €4,00 as soon as the first hour has passed. The function relating parking charges to parking time will be called <em>discontinuous</em> at each hour.</p>\n<p>Most functions that we encounter have domains that are intervals, or unions of separate intervals. A point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\)</span> in the domain of such a function is called an <em>interior point</em> of the domain if it belongs to some open interval contained in the domain. If it is not an interior point, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\)</span> is called an <em>endpoint</em> of the domain. For example, the domain of the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\)</span> is the closed interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[-2,2\\right]\\)</span>, which consists of interior points in the interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left]-2,2\\right[\\)</span>, a left endpoint <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-2\\)</span>, and a right endpoint <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2\\)</span>. The domain of the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\\)</span> is the union of open intervals <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left]-\\infty, 0\\right[\\cup\\left]0,\\infty\\right[\\)</span> and consists entirely of interior points. Note that although <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> is an endpoint of each of those intervals, it does not belong to the domain of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> and so is not an endpoint of that domain.</p>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<h5>Continuity of a function at a point</h5>\n<p>A function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span>, defined on an open interval containing the point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>, an interior point, is said to be continuous at the point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span> if</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)\\,;\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>that is,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\forall \\varepsilon&gt;0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)&gt;0:\\left|x-c\\right|&lt;\\delta\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>If either <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> fails to exist or it exists but is not equal to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)\\)</span>, then we will say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is discontinuous at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>In graphical terms, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continuous at an interior point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span> of its domain if its graph has no break in it at the point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(c,f\\left(x\\right)\\right)\\)</span>; in other words, if you can draw the graph through that point without lifting your pen from the paper.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 70;\nconst width = 8.5 * scale;\nconst height = 2.5 * scale;\nconst ymid = 2 * scale;\nconst red = \"#ff0000\";\nconst black = \"#000000\";\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width,\n    height,\n    wide: true,\n    caption: \"\\\\(f\\\\) is continuous at \\\\(c\\\\); \\\\(\\\\lim_{x → c} g(x) ≠ g(c)\\\\); \\\\(\\\\lim_{x → c} h(x)\\\\) does not exist.\",\n});\n\nfunction drawAxes(shiftX, xmid) {\n    const panelWidth = 2.5 * scale;\n    const axisEnd = shiftX + panelWidth;\n\n    figure.line()\n        .x1(shiftX).y1(ymid).x2(axisEnd - 3).y2(ymid)\n        .stroke(black);\n    figure.polygon()\n        .points(`${axisEnd},${ymid} ${axisEnd - 7},${ymid - 4} ${axisEnd - 7},${ymid + 4}`)\n        .fill(black);\n\n    figure.line()\n        .x1(xmid).y1(height).x2(xmid).y2(3)\n        .stroke(black);\n    figure.polygon()\n        .points(`${xmid},0 ${xmid - 4},7 ${xmid + 4},7`)\n        .fill(black);\n\n    const tickX = xmid + scale;\n    figure.line()\n        .x1(tickX).y1(ymid - 3).x2(tickX).y2(ymid + 3)\n        .stroke(black);\n\n    figure.label(\"O\", { x: xmid - 16, y: ymid, latex: true, width: 14, height: 18 });\n    figure.label(\"x\", { x: axisEnd - 16, y: ymid - 20, latex: true, width: 14, height: 18 });\n    figure.label(\"y\", { x: xmid + 2, y: -2, latex: true, width: 14, height: 22 });\n    figure.label(\"c\", { x: tickX - 7, y: ymid, latex: true, width: 14, height: 18 });\n}\n\nfunction plot(xmid, f, start, end, samples) {\n    const points = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < samples; i += 1) {\n        const x = start + (end - start) * i / (samples - 1);\n        points.push(`${xmid + scale * x},${ymid - scale * f(x)}`);\n    }\n    figure.polyline()\n        .points(points.join(\" \"))\n        .fill(\"none\")\n        .stroke(red)\n        .strokeWidth(1);\n}\n\nfunction mark(xmid, x, y, fill) {\n    figure.circle()\n        .cx(xmid + scale * x)\n        .cy(ymid - scale * y)\n        .r(3)\n        .fill(fill)\n        .stroke(red);\n}\n\nconst f = (x) => 0.3 * x ** 2 + 0.1;\nconst h = (x) => -2 * (x - 1.5) ** 2 + 1.5;\nconst origins = [0.5 * scale, 3.5 * scale, 6.5 * scale];\n\ndrawAxes(0, origins[0]);\nplot(origins[0], f, -0.5, 2, 251);\nmark(origins[0], 1, f(1), red);\nfigure.label(String.raw`y=f\\left(x\\right)`, {\n    x: origins[0] + scale - 28, y: 0, width: 60, height: 22, latex: true,\n});\n\ndrawAxes(3 * scale, origins[1]);\nplot(origins[1], f, -0.5, 2, 251);\nfigure.label(String.raw`y=g\\left(x\\right)`, {\n    x: origins[1] + scale - 28, y: 0, width: 60, height: 22, latex: true,\n});\nmark(origins[1], 1, 0.4, \"#ffffff\");\nmark(origins[1], 1, 1, red);\n\ndrawAxes(6 * scale, origins[2]);\nplot(origins[2], f, -0.5, 1, 151);\nplot(origins[2], h, 1, 2, 101);\nfigure.label(String.raw`y=h\\left(x\\right)`, {\n    x: origins[2] + scale - 28, y: 0, width: 60, height: 22, latex: true,\n});\nmark(origins[2], 1, 0.4, \"#ffffff\");\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"Oxycy=f(x)Oxycy=g(x)Oxycy=h(x)f is continuous at c; limx→c⁡g(x)≠g(c); limx→c⁡h(x) does not exist.","resultHTML":"<figure class=\"wide\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"595\" height=\"175\" viewBox=\"0 0 595 175\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"172\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"175,140 168,136 168,144\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"3\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"35,0 31,7 39,7\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"137\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"143\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"19\" y=\"140\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\"><div xmlns=\"htt