e=\"#ff0000\"></circle><polyline points=\"240,100 240.79999999999998,99.20000000000002 241.6,98.4 242.39999999999998,97.60000000000002 243.2,96.8 244,96.00000000000001 244.8,95.19999999999999 245.6,94.4 246.4,93.6 247.2,92.80000000000001 248,92 248.79999999999998,91.20000000000002 249.60000000000002,90.39999999999999 250.39999999999998,89.60000000000001 251.20000000000002,88.79999999999998 252,88 252.8,87.19999999999999 253.6,86.4 254.4,85.6 255.2,84.80000000000001 256,83.99999999999999 256.8,83.2 257.6,82.39999999999998 258.4,81.6 259.20000000000005,80.79999999999998 260,80 260.79999999999995,79.20000000000002 261.6,78.4 262.40000000000003,77.59999999999998 263.2,76.8 264,76.00000000000001 264.8,75.19999999999999 265.6,74.4 266.4,73.6 267.2,72.80000000000001 268,72 268.79999999999995,71.20000000000002 269.6,70.39999999999999 270.4,69.60000000000001 271.20000000000005,68.79999999999998 272,68 272.8,67.19999999999999 273.6,66.4 274.4,65.6 275.2,64.80000000000001 276,63.999999999999986 276.8,63.2 277.59999999999997,62.40000000000002 278.4,61.6 279.20000000000005,60.79999999999998 280,60 280.79999999999995,59.20000000000002 281.6,58.4 282.40000000000003,57.59999999999998 283.2,56.8 284,56.000000000000014 284.8,55.19999999999999 285.6,54.400000000000006 286.4,53.599999999999994 287.2,52.80000000000001 288,52 288.79999999999995,51.20000000000002 289.6,50.39999999999999 290.4,49.60000000000001 291.20000000000005,48.79999999999998 292,48 292.8,47.19999999999999 293.6,46.400000000000006 294.4,45.599999999999994 295.2,44.80000000000001 296,43.999999999999986 296.8,43.2 297.59999999999997,42.40000000000002 298.4,41.599999999999994 299.20000000000005,40.79999999999998 300,40 300.79999999999995,39.20000000000002 301.6,38.400000000000006 302.40000000000003,37.59999999999998 303.2,36.8 304,36.000000000000014 304.8,35.19999999999999 305.59999999999997,34.400000000000006 306.4,33.599999999999994 307.2,32.80000000000001 308,32 308.8,31.200000000000017 309.6,30.39999999999999 310.4,29.60000000000001 311.2,28.799999999999983 312,28 312.8,27.19999999999999 313.6,26.400000000000006 314.40000000000003,25.599999999999994 315.2,24.80000000000001 316,23.999999999999986 316.8,23.200000000000003 317.59999999999997,22.40000000000002 318.4,21.599999999999994 319.20000000000005,20.799999999999983 320,20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><circle cx=\"320\" cy=\"20\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><polyline points=\"320,65 320.79999999999995,65.19999999999999 321.6,65.4 322.4,65.6 323.2,65.8 324,66 324.8,66.2 325.59999999999997,66.4 326.4,66.6 327.2,66.8 328,67 328.8,67.19999999999999 329.6,67.4 330.4,67.6 331.2,67.80000000000001 332,68 332.8,68.2 333.6,68.4 334.40000000000003,68.6 335.2,68.8 336,69 336.8,69.2 337.6,69.4 338.4,69.6 339.20000000000005,69.80000000000001 340,70 340.79999999999995,70.19999999999999 341.6,70.4 342.40000000000003,70.60000000000001 343.2,70.8 344,71 344.8,71.2 345.59999999999997,71.4 346.4,71.6 347.2,71.8 348,72 348.8,72.19999999999999 349.6,72.4 350.4,72.6 351.2,72.80000000000001 352,73 352.8,73.2 353.6,73.4 354.40000000000003,73.6 355.2,73.8 356,74 356.8,74.2 357.59999999999997,74.39999999999999 358.4,74.6 359.20000000000005,74.80000000000001 360,75 360.79999999999995,75.19999999999999 361.59999999999997,75.39999999999999 362.40000000000003,75.60000000000001 363.2,75.8 364,76 364.80000000000007,76.20000000000002 365.6,76.4 366.4,76.6 367.2,76.8 368,77 368.8,77.2 369.6,77.4 370.4,77.6 371.2,77.8 372,78 372.8,78.2 373.6,78.4 374.4,78.6 375.19999999999993,78.79999999999998 376,79 376.8,79.2 377.59999999999997,79.39999999999999 378.40000000000003,79.60000000000001 379.20000000000005,79.80000000000001 380,80 380.79999999999995,80.19999999999999 381.59999999999997,80.39999999999999 382.40000000000003,80.60000000000001 383.2,80.8 384,81 384.80000000000007,81.20000000000002 385.6,81.4 386.4,81.6 387.2,81.8 388,82 388.8,82.2 389.6,82.4 390.4,82.6 391.2,82.8 392,83 392.8,83.2 393.6,83.4 394.4,83.6 395.19999999999993,83.79999999999998 396,84 396.8,84.2 397.59999999999997,84.39999999999999 398.40000000000003,84.60000000000001 399.20000000000005,84.80000000000001 400,85\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"400\" cy=\"85\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><circle cx=\"320\" cy=\"65\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"24\" y=\"120\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption><math><mi>f</mi></math> is continuous on the intervals <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, <math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>b</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo></mrow></math>, <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>d</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"## Continuous Functions\n\n::: definition \"Continuous function\"\nWe say that ``f`` is a *continuous function* if ``f`` is continuous at every point of its domain.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example\n\n    The function ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{x}`` is a continuous function. Its domain is ``\\left[0,\\infty\\right[``. It is continuous at the left endpoint ``0`` because it is right continuous there. Also, ``f`` is continuous at every number ``c>0`` since ``\\lim_{x\\to c}\\sqrt x=\\sqrt c``.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example\n\n    The function ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}`` is also a continuous function. This may seem wrong to you at first glance because its graph is broken at ``x=0``. However, the number ``0`` is not in the domain of ``g``, so we will prefer to say that ``g`` is undefined rather than discontinuous there.\n\nThe following functions are continuous wherever they are defined:\n1. all polynomials;\n2. all rational functions;\n3. all rational powers ``x^\\frac{m}{n}=\\sqrt[n]{x^m}``;\n4. the sine, cosine, tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent functions; and\n5. the absolute value function ``\\left|x\\right|``.\n\nCorollary 4 of this chapter assures us that every polynomial is continuous everywhere on the real line, and every rational function is continuous everywhere on its domain (which consists of all real numbers except the finitely many where its denominator is zero). If ``m`` and ``n`` are integers and ``n \\neq 0``, the rational power function ``x^\\frac{m}{n}`` is defined for all positive numbers ``x``, and also for all negative numbers ``x`` if ``n`` is odd. The domain includes ``0`` if and only if ``\\frac{m}{n} \\ge 0``.\n\nThe following theorems show that if we combine continuous functions in various ways, the results will be continuous.\n\n::: proposition\nIf ``f`` and ``g`` are continuous at the point ``c``, then so are ``f+g``, ``f-g``, ``fg``, and, if ``g\\left(c\\right)\\neq0``, ``\\displaystyle\\frac{f}{g}``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nThis is just a restatement of various rules for combining limits; for example,\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)\\right)\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)g\\left(c\\right)\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n::: proposition\nIf ``f`` is a continuous function at the point ``L`` and if ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)=L``, then we have\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(L\\right)=f\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)\\right)\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n::: proof\nLet ``\\varepsilon >0`` be given.\n\nSince ``f`` is continuous at ``L``, there exists ``\\kappa>0`` such that ``\\left|f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-f\\left(L\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon`` whenever ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\kappa``.\n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)=L``, there exists ``\\delta>0`` such that if ``0<\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\kappa``.\n\nHence, if ``0<\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-f\\left(L\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon``, and ``\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(L\\right)``.\n:::","output":"","result":"## Continuous Functions\n\n::: definition \"Continuous function\"\nWe say that ``f`` is a *continuous function* if ``f`` is continuous at every point of its domain.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example\n\n    The function ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{x}`` is a continuous function. Its domain is ``\\left[0,\\infty\\right[``. It is continuous at the left endpoint ``0`` because it is right continuous there. Also, ``f`` is continuous at every number ``c>0`` since ``\\lim_{x\\to c}\\sqrt x=\\sqrt c``.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example\n\n    The function ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}`` is also a continuous function. This may seem wrong to you at first glance because its graph is broken at ``x=0``. However, the number ``0`` is not in the domain of ``g``, so we will prefer to say that ``g`` is undefined rather than discontinuous there.\n\nThe following functions are continuous wherever they are defined:\n1. all polynomials;\n2. all rational functions;\n3. all rational powers ``x^\\frac{m}{n}=\\sqrt[n]{x^m}``;\n4. the sine, cosine, tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent functions; and\n5. the absolute value function ``\\left|x\\right|``.\n\nCorollary 4 of this chapter assures us that every polynomial is continuous everywhere on the real line, and every rational function is continuous everywhere on its domain (which consists of all real numbers except the finitely many where its denominator is zero). If ``m`` and ``n`` are integers and ``n \\neq 0``, the rational power function ``x^\\frac{m}{n}`` is defined for all positive numbers ``x``, and also for all negative numbers ``x`` if ``n`` is odd. The domain includes ``0`` if and only if ``\\frac{m}{n} \\ge 0``.\n\nThe following theorems show that if we combine continuous functions in various ways, the results will be continuous.\n\n::: proposition\nIf ``f`` and ``g`` are continuous at the point ``c``, then so are ``f+g``, ``f-g``, ``fg``, and, if ``g\\left(c\\right)\\neq0``, ``\\displaystyle\\frac{f}{g}``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nThis is just a restatement of various rules for combining limits; for example,\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)\\right)\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)g\\left(c\\right)\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n::: proposition\nIf ``f`` is a continuous function at the point ``L`` and if ``\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)=L``, then we have\n```math\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(L\\right)=f\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)\\right)\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n::: proof\nLet ``\\varepsilon >0`` be given.\n\nSince ``f`` is continuous at ``L``, there exists ``\\kappa>0`` such that ``\\left|f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-f\\left(L\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon`` whenever ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\kappa``.\n\nSince ``\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)=L``, there exists ``\\delta>0`` such that if ``0<\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\kappa``.\n\nHence, if ``0<\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-f\\left(L\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon``, and ``\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(L\\right)``.\n:::","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2666-continuous-functions\">\n<h1>Continuous Functions</h1>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<h5>Continuous function</h5>\n<p>We say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is a <em>continuous function</em> if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continuous at every point of its domain.</p>\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<p>The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{x}\\)</span> is a continuous function. Its domain is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[0,\\infty\\right[\\)</span>. It is continuous at the left endpoint <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> because it is right continuous there. Also, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continuous at every number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span> since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to c}\\sqrt x=\\sqrt c\\)</span>.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<p>The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\\)</span> is also a continuous function. This may seem wrong to you at first glance because its graph is broken at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=0\\)</span>. However, the number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> is not in the domain of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span>, so we will prefer to say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> is undefined rather than discontinuous there.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>The following functions are continuous wherever they are defined:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>all polynomials;</li>\n<li>all rational functions;</li>\n<li>all rational powers <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x^\\frac{m}{n}=\\sqrt[n]{x^m}\\)</span>;</li>\n<li>the sine, cosine, tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent functions; and</li>\n<li>the absolute value function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x\\right|\\)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Corollary 4 of this chapter assures us that every polynomial is continuous everywhere on the real line, and every rational function is continuous everywhere on its domain (which consists of all real numbers except the finitely many where its denominator is zero). If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> are integers and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n \\neq 0\\)</span>, the rational power function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x^\\frac{m}{n}\\)</span> is defined for all positive numbers <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>, and also for all negative numbers <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n\\)</span> is odd. The domain includes <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> if and only if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{m}{n} \\ge 0\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>The following theorems show that if we combine continuous functions in various ways, the results will be continuous.</p>\n<div data-type=\"proposition\">\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> are continuous at the point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>, then so are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f+g\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f-g\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(fg\\)</span>, and, if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(c\\right)\\neq0\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{f}{g}\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>This is just a restatement of various rules for combining limits; for example,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)\\right)\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)g\\left(c\\right)\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proposition\">\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is a continuous function at the point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> and if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span>, then we have</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(L\\right)=f\\left(\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)\\right)\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\varepsilon &gt;0\\)</span> be given.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continuous at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span>, there exists <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\kappa&gt;0\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-f\\left(L\\right)\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)</span> whenever <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\kappa\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to c}g\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span>, there exists <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)</span> such that if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0&lt;\\left|x-c\\right|&lt;\\delta\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|&lt;\\kappa\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Hence, if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0&lt;\\left|x-c\\right|&lt;\\delta\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-f\\left(L\\right)\\right|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=f\\left(L\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Continuous Extensions\n\nAs we have seen a rational function may have a limit even at a point where its denominator is zero. If ``f\\left(c\\right)`` is not defined, but ``\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` exists, we can define a new function ``F\\left(x\\right)`` by\n```math\n    F\\left(x\\right)=\\begin{cases}\n    f\\left(x\\right)&\\textrm{if }x\\textrm{ is in the domain of }f\\\\\n    L&\\textrm{if }x=c\\,.\n    \\end{cases}\n```\n\n``F\\left(x\\right)`` is continuous at ``x=c``. It is called the *continuous extension* of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` to ``x=c``. For rational functions, continuous extensions are usually found by cancelling common factors.\n\n!!! example \"Show that ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-x}{x^2-1}`` has a continuous extension to ``x=1``, and find that extension.\"\n\n    Although ``f\\left(1\\right)`` is not defined, if ``x\\neq 1`` we have\n    ```math\n    f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-x}{x^2-1}=\\frac{x\\left(x-1\\right)}{\\left(x+1\\right)\\left(x-1\\right)}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    The function ``\\displaystyle F\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x}{x+1}`` is equal to ``f\\left(x\\right)`` for ``x\\neq 1`` but is also continuous at ``x=1``, having there the value ``\\frac{1}{2}``. The continuous extension of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` to ``x=1`` is ``F\\left(x\\right)``. It has the same graph as ``f\\left(x\\right)`` except with no hole at ``\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)``.\n\nIf a function ``f`` is undefined or discontinuous at a point ``c`` but can be (re)defined at that single point so that it becomes continuous there, then we say that ``f`` has a *removable discontinuity* at ``c``. The function ``f`` in the above example has a removable discontinuity at ``x=1``. To remove it, define ``f\\left(1\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}``.\n\n!!! example\n\n    The function ``\\displaystyle g\\left(x\\right)=\\begin{cases}x&\\textrm{if}\\ x\\neq2\\\\1&\\textrm{if }x=2\\end{cases}`` has a removable discontinuity at ``x=2``. To remove it, redefine ``g\\left(2\\right)=2``.","output":"","result":"## Continuous Extensions\n\nAs we have seen a rational function may have a limit even at a point where its denominator is zero. If ``f\\left(c\\right)`` is not defined, but ``\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` exists, we can define a new function ``F\\left(x\\right)`` by\n```math\n    F\\left(x\\right)=\\begin{cases}\n    f\\left(x\\right)&\\textrm{if }x\\textrm{ is in the domain of }f\\\\\n    L&\\textrm{if }x=c\\,.\n    \\end{cases}\n```\n\n``F\\left(x\\right)`` is continuous at ``x=c``. It is called the *continuous extension* of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` to ``x=c``. For rational functions, continuous extensions are usually found by cancelling common factors.\n\n!!! example \"Show that ``f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-x}{x^2-1}`` has a continuous extension to ``x=1``, and find that extension.\"\n\n    Although ``f\\left(1\\right)`` is not defined, if ``x\\neq 1`` we have\n    ```math\n    f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-x}{x^2-1}=\\frac{x\\left(x-1\\right)}{\\left(x+1\\right)\\left(x-1\\right)}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    The function ``\\displaystyle F\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x}{x+1}`` is equal to ``f\\left(x\\right)`` for ``x\\neq 1`` but is also continuous at ``x=1``, having there the value ``\\frac{1}{2}``. The continuous extension of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` to ``x=1`` is ``F\\left(x\\right)``. It has the same graph as ``f\\left(x\\right)`` except with no hole at ``\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)``.\n\nIf a function ``f`` is undefined or discontinuous at a point ``c`` but can be (re)defined at that single point so that it becomes continuous there, then we say that ``f`` has a *removable discontinuity* at ``c``. The function ``f`` in the above example has a removable discontinuity at ``x=1``. To remove it, define ``f\\left(1\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}``.\n\n!!! example\n\n    The function ``\\displaystyle g\\left(x\\right)=\\begin{cases}x&\\textrm{if}\\ x\\neq2\\\\1&\\textrm{if }x=2\\end{cases}`` has a removable discontinuity at ``x=2``. To remove it, redefine ``g\\left(2\\right)=2``.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2667-continuous-extensions\">\n<h1>Continuous Extensions</h1>\n<p>As we have seen a rational function may have a limit even at a point where its denominator is zero. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)\\)</span> is not defined, but <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lim_{x\\to c}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\)</span> exists, we can define a new function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> by</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n    F\\left(x\\right)=\\begin{cases}\n    f\\left(x\\right)&amp;\\textrm{if }x\\textrm{ is in the domain of }f\\\\\n    L&amp;\\textrm{if }x=c\\,.\n    \\end{cases}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> is continuous at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=c\\)</span>. It is called the <em>continuous extension</em> of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=c\\)</span>. For rational functions, continuous extensions are usually found by cancelling common factors.</p>\n<div data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-x}{x^2-1}\\)</span> has a continuous extension to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>, and find that extension.</h5>\n<p>Although <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(1\\right)\\)</span> is not defined, if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\neq 1\\)</span> we have</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nf\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x^2-x}{x^2-1}=\\frac{x\\left(x-1\\right)}{\\left(x+1\\right)\\left(x-1\\right)}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle F\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\)</span> is equal to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\neq 1\\)</span> but is also continuous at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>, having there the value <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)</span>. The continuous extension of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F\\left(x\\right)\\)</span>. It has the same graph as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> except with no hole at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>If a function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is undefined or discontinuous at a point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span> but can be (re)defined at that single point so that it becomes continuous there, then we say that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has a <em>removable discontinuity</em> at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>. The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> in the above example has a removable discontinuity at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=1\\)</span>. To remove it, define <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(1\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\)</span>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"example\">\n<p>The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle g\\left(x\\right)=\\begin{cases}x&amp;\\textrm{if}\\ x\\neq2\\\\1&amp;\\textrm{if }x=2\\end{cases}\\)</span> has a removable discontinuity at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=2\\)</span>. To remove it, redefine <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(2\\right)=2\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The Intermediate-Value Theorem\n\nContinuous functions that are defined on closed, finite intervals have special properties that make them particularly useful in mathematics and its applications. We will discuss two of these properties here. Although they may appear obvious, these properties are much more subtle than the results about limits stated earlier in this chapter; their proofs require a careful study of the implications of the completeness property of the real numbers and are based on the Nested Intervals theorem.\n\nThe first property of a continuous function defined on a closed, finite interval is that the function takes on all real values between any two of its values. This property is called the intermediate-value property.","output":"","result":"## The Intermediate-Value Theorem\n\nContinuous functions that are defined on closed, finite intervals have special properties that make them particularly useful in mathematics and its applications. We will discuss two of these properties here. Although they may appear obvious, these properties are much more subtle than the results about limits stated earlier in this chapter; their proofs require a careful study of the implications of the completeness property of the real numbers and are based on the Nested Intervals theorem.\n\nThe first property of a continuous function defined on a closed, finite interval is that the function takes on all real values between any two of its values. This property is called the intermediate-value property.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2668-the-intermediatevalue-theorem\">\n<h1>The Intermediate-Value Theorem</h1>\n<p>Continuous functions that are defined on closed, finite intervals have special properties that make them particularly useful in mathematics and its applications. We will discuss two of these properties here. Although they may appear obvious, these properties are much more subtle than the results about limits stated earlier in this chapter; their proofs require a careful study of the implications of the completeness property of the real numbers and are based on the Nested Intervals theorem.</p>\n<p>The first property of a continuous function defined on a closed, finite interval is that the function takes on all real values between any two of its values. This property is called the intermediate-value property.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 60;\nconst width = 5 * scale;\nconst height = 4.5 * scale;\nconst xmid = scale;\nconst ymid = 4 * scale;\nconst shiftX = 0.5 * scale;\nconst axisWidth = 4.5 * scale;\nconst fontX = 14;\nconst fontY = 22;\nconst red = \"#ff0000\";\nconst blue = \"#4169e1\";\nconst black = \"#000000\";\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width,\n    height,\n    caption: \"The continuous function \\\\(f\\\\) takes on the value \\\\(s\\\\) at some point \\\\(c\\\\) between \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\).\",\n});\n\nconst f = (x) => Math.sin(4 * x - 2) + (x - 0.5) + 0.5;\nconst x = 2.075;\nconst ymin = f(0.5);\nconst y = f(x);\nconst ymax = f(3.5);\nconst mapX = (value) => xmid + scale * value;\nconst mapY = (value) => ymid - scale * value;\n\nfunction line(x1, y1, x2, y2, color = black, dashed = false) {\n    const element = figure.line()\n        .x1(x1).y1(y1).x2(x2).y2(y2)\n        .stroke(color);\n    if (dashed) element.setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n}\n\nfunction point(px, py, color, fill = color) {\n    figure.circle().cx(px).cy(py).r(3).fill(fill).stroke(color);\n}\n\n// Shifted axes, arrowheads, and the tick marks.\nline(shiftX, ymid, shiftX + axisWidth - 3, ymid);\nfigure.polygon()\n    .points(`${shiftX + axisWidth},${ymid} ${shiftX + axisWidth - 7},${ymid - 4} ${shiftX + axisWidth - 7},${ymid + 4}`)\n    .fill(black);\nline(xmid, height, xmid, 3);\nfigure.polygon()\n    .points(`${xmid},0 ${xmid - 4},7 ${xmid + 4},7`)\n    .fill(black);\n\nfor (const tick of [0.5, x, 3.5]) {\n    line(mapX(tick), ymid - 3, mapX(tick), ymid + 3);\n}\nfor (const tick of [ymin, y, ymax]) {\n    line(xmid - 3, mapY(tick), xmid + 3, mapY(tick));\n}\n\n// Axis labels and tick labels.\nfigure.label(\"O\", { x: xmid - 16, y: ymid, latex: true, width: 14, height: fontY });\nfigure.label(\"x\", { x: shiftX + axisWidth - 16, y: ymid - 20, latex: true, width: 14, height: fontY });\nfigure.label(\"y\", { x: xmid + 2, y: -2, latex: true, width: 14, height: fontY });\n\nfor (const [tick, label] of [[0.5, \"a\"], [x, \"c\"], [3.5, \"b\"]]) {\n    figure.label(label, {\n        x: mapX(tick) - fontX / 2,\n        y: ymid,\n        latex: true,\n        width: fontX,\n        height: fontY,\n    });\n}\nfor (const [tick, label, labelWidth] of [\n    [ymin, String.raw`f\\left(a\\right)`, 3 * fontX],\n    [y, \"s\", 1.5 * fontX],\n    [ymax, String.raw`f\\left(b\\right)`, 3 * fontX],\n]) {\n    figure.label(label, {\n        x: xmid - labelWidth,\n        y: mapY(tick) - fontY / 2,\n        latex: true,\n        width: labelWidth,\n        height: fontY,\n    });\n}\n\n// Plot f and its endpoint markers.\nconst curvePoints = [];\nfor (let index = 0; index <= 300; index += 1) {\n    const value = 0.5 + index / 100;\n    curvePoints.push(`${mapX(value)},${mapY(f(value))}`);\n}\nfigure.polyline().points(curvePoints.join(\" \")).fill(\"none\").stroke(red).strokeWidth(1);\npoint(mapX(0.5), mapY(ymin), red);\npoint(mapX(3.5), mapY(ymax), red);\nfigure.label(String.raw`y=f\\left(x\\right)`, {\n    x: xmid + scale - 2 * fontX,\n    y: 0,\n    width: 4 * fontX,\n    height: fontY,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\n// Mark s = g(c), with guide lines.\npoint(mapX(x), mapY(y), blue);\nline(xmid, mapY(y), mapX(3.5), mapY(y), blue);\nline(mapX(x), ymid, mapX(x), mapY(y), blue);\n\n// Dashed guides for f(a) and f(b).\nline(xmid, mapY(ymin), mapX(0.5), mapY(ymin), black, true);\nline(mapX(0.5), ymid, mapX(0.5), mapY(ymin), black, true);\nline(xmid, mapY(ymax), mapX(3.5), mapY(ymax), black, true);\nline(mapX(3.5), ymid, mapX(3.5), mapY(ymax), black, true);\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"Oxyacbf(a)sf(b)y=f(x)The continuous function f takes on the value s at some point c between a and b.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"300\" height=\"270\" viewBox=\"0 0 300 270\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"297\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"300,240 293,236 293,244\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"3\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"60,0 56,7 64,7\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"243\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"184.5\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"184.5\" y2=\"243\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"243\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"57\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"63\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"57\" y1=\"114.49116597093895\" x2=\"63\" y2=\"114.49116597093895\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"57\" y1=\"62.19437508002608\" x2=\"63\" y2=\"62.19437508002608\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"44\" y=\"240\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"284\" y=\"220\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"62\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"83\" y=\"240\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"177.5\" y=\"240\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"263\" y=\"240\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>b</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"18\" y=\"199\" width=\"42\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"39\" y=\"103.49116597093895\" width=\"21\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>s</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"18\" y=\"51.19437508002608\" width=\"42\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><polyline points=\"90,210 90.6,207.00063994880196 91.2,204.00511836184964 91.8,201.01726756266484 92.4,198.04090760314523 93,195.07984015229633 93.6,192.1378424143719 94.2,189.21866108615316 94.8,186.32600636303295 95.4,183.4635460034946 96,180.63489946148098 96.6,177.84363209604004 97.2,175.09324946751104 97.8,172.3871917293758 98.4,169.728828124747 99,167.12145159629785 99.6,164.56827351825646 100.2,162.07241855889188 100.8,159.63691968171162 101.4,157.26471329336692 102,154.95863454602866 102.6,152.72141280174844 103.19999999999999,150.55566726606185 103.8,148.46390279781804 104.4,146.4485059019401 105,144.5117409115262 105.6,142.6557463653997 106.2,140.88253158690316 106.80000000000001,139.19397346940968 107.4,137.59181347369397 108,136.07765484196642 108.6,134.65296003302765 109.2,133.31904838264649 109.80000000000001,132.07709399290408 110.4,130.928123853881 111,129.8730162006924 111.6,128.91249910849882 112.2,128.04714932774158 112.8,127.27739136146502 113.4,126.60349678620203 114,126.0255838175097 114.6,125.54361712084959 115.19999999999999,125.1574078