9767292083985 249,106.3665252967684 249.60000000000002,106.64680304311759 250.2,106.8370979032758 250.8,106.93614557405408 251.4,106.94282772830135 252,106.85617380398946 252.6,106.67536255690533 253.20000000000002,106.39972337446642 253.8,106.02873734855791 254.4,105.56203810567496 255,104.9994123930422 255.6,104.34080041977259 256.2,103.58629595252009 256.79999999999995,102.7361461654732 257.4,101.79075124492863 258,100.75066374907905 258.6,99.6165877240374 259.2,98.3893775775137 259.8,97.0700367119446 260.4,95.65971591926387 261,94.15971153988056 261.6,92.571463388811 262.20000000000005,90.89655245228181 262.79999999999995,89.13669835848947 263.4,87.29375662656156 264,85.36971569812255 264.6,83.36669375621074 265.2,81.28693533663801 265.8,79.13280773721056 266.4,76.90679723055811 267,74.61150508662735 267.6,72.24964341120571 268.20000000000005,69.82403080712993 268.8,67.33758786512345 269.4,64.79333249147362 270,62.19437508002608\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></polyline><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"210\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><circle cx=\"270\" cy=\"62.19437508002608\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"92\" y=\"0\" width=\"56\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><circle cx=\"184.5\" cy=\"114.49116597093895\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#4169e1\" stroke=\"#4169e1\"></circle><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"114.49116597093895\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"114.49116597093895\" stroke=\"#4169e1\"></line><line x1=\"184.5\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"184.5\" y2=\"114.49116597093895\" stroke=\"#4169e1\"></line><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"62.19437508002608\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"62.19437508002608\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"62.19437508002608\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line></svg><figcaption>The continuous function <math><mi>f</mi></math> takes on the value <math><mi>s</mi></math> at some point <math><mi>c</mi></math> between <math><mi>a</mi></math> and <math><mi>b</mi></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"The figure shows a typical situation. The points ``\\left(a, f\\left(a\\right)\\right)`` and ``\\left(b, f\\left(b\\right)\\right)`` are on opposite sides of the horizontal line ``y=s``. Being unbroken, the graph ``y=f\\left(x\\right)`` must cross this line in order to go from one point to the other. In the figure, it crosses the line only once, at ``x=c``. If the line ``y=s`` were somewhat lower or higher, there might have been three crossings and three possible values for ``c``.\n\nWe need the following lemma to prove the intermediate-value property.\n\n::: lemma \"Aura theorem\"\nLet ``f`` be continuous at ``c``.\n\n1. If ``f\\left(c\\right)>0``, then ``f\\left(x\\right)>0`` for all ``x`` in some open interval containing ``c``.\n2. If ``f\\left(c\\right)<0``, then ``f\\left(x\\right)<0`` for all ``x`` in some open interval containing ``c``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nLet ``f\\left(c\\right)>0``.\n\nThen, corresponding to ``\\displaystyle\\varepsilon = \\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}>0`` there exists a corresponding ``\\delta>0`` such that ``\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta`` implies\n```math\n\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)\\right|<\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2} \\iff -\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}<f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)<\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2} \\iff 0<\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}<f\\left(x\\right)<\\frac{3f\\left(c\\right)}{2}\\,.\n```\nHence, ``f\\left(x\\right)>0`` for all ``x\\in\\left]c-\\delta,c+\\delta\\right[``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! exercise \"Prove the second part of the Aura theorem.\"\n\nWe will first prove a special case from which the general case follows easily.\n\n::: theorem \"Bolzano's theorem\"\nLet ``f`` be a continuous function defined on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``. If ``f\\left(a\\right)<0`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)>0``, then there exists ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n:::\n\n::: proof \"by contradiction\"\nLet ``I_0=\\left[a_0, b_0\\right]=\\left[a,b\\right]``. \n\nIf ``\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2}\\right)=0``, we are done. Otherwise, ``f`` changes sign on either ``\\displaystyle\\left[a_0,\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2}\\right]`` or ``\\displaystyle\\left[\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2},b_0\\right]``.\n\nLet ``I_1=\\left[a_1, b_1\\right]`` be the subinterval on which ``f`` changes sign and repeat.\n\nBy the Nested Intervals theorem, ``\\bigcap_{n\\in \\mathbb{N}}I_n=\\left\\{c\\right\\}``, where ``c=\\sup\\left\\{a_n\\right\\}=\\inf\\left\\{b_n\\right\\}``.\n\nSuppose ``f\\left(c\\right)>0``. By the Aura theorem, ``f`` must be positive on an open interval containing ``c``. Since ``c=\\sup\\left\\{a_n\\right\\}``, by the Capture theorem this open interval must contain some ``a_m``. But ``f\\left(a_m\\right)<0`` which is a contradiction.\n\nSuppose ``f\\left(c\\right)<0``. By the Aura theorem, ``f`` must be negative on an open interval containing ``c``. Since ``c=\\inf\\left\\{b_n\\right\\}``, by the Capture theorem this open interval must contain some ``b_m``. But ``f\\left(b_m\\right)>0`` which is a contradiction.\n\nHence, ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n:::\n\nIf ``f\\left(a\\right)>0`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)<0``, then ``g=-f`` satisfies the hypotheses of Bolzano's theorem and therefore ``g\\left(c\\right)=-f\\left(c\\right)=0`` for some ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[``, and hence ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n\n::: theorem \"Intermediate-value theorem\"\nIf ``f`` is continuous on the interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` and if ``s`` is a number between ``f\\left(a\\right)`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)``, then there exists a number ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=s``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nLet ``s`` be a number between ``f\\left(a\\right)`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)``.\n\nThe function ``g\\left(x\\right)=f\\left(x\\right)-s`` is continuous and satifies the hypotheses of Bolzano's theorem, so there exists some ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[`` such that ``g\\left(c\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)-s=0``.\n\nHence, ``f\\left(c\\right)=s``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Show that the equation ``x^3-x-1=0`` has a solution in the interval ``\\left[1,2\\right]``.\"\n\n    The function ``f\\left(x\\right)=x^3-x-1`` is a polynomial and is therefore continuous everywhere.\n\n    Now ``f\\left(1\\right)=-1`` and ``f\\left(2\\right)=5``. Since ``0`` lies between ``-1`` and ``5``, the Intermediate-value theorem assures us that there must be a number ``c\\in\\left[1,2\\right]`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n\nOne method for finding a zero of a function that is continuous and changes sign on an interval involves bisecting the interval many times, each time determining which half of the previous interval must contain the root, because the function has opposite signs at the two ends of that half.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Solve the equation ``x^3-x-1=0`` correct to ``3`` decimal places by successive bisection.\"\n\n    The root is ``1.325`` rounded to ``3`` decimal places.","output":"","result":"The figure shows a typical situation. The points ``\\left(a, f\\left(a\\right)\\right)`` and ``\\left(b, f\\left(b\\right)\\right)`` are on opposite sides of the horizontal line ``y=s``. Being unbroken, the graph ``y=f\\left(x\\right)`` must cross this line in order to go from one point to the other. In the figure, it crosses the line only once, at ``x=c``. If the line ``y=s`` were somewhat lower or higher, there might have been three crossings and three possible values for ``c``.\n\nWe need the following lemma to prove the intermediate-value property.\n\n::: lemma \"Aura theorem\"\nLet ``f`` be continuous at ``c``.\n\n1. If ``f\\left(c\\right)>0``, then ``f\\left(x\\right)>0`` for all ``x`` in some open interval containing ``c``.\n2. If ``f\\left(c\\right)<0``, then ``f\\left(x\\right)<0`` for all ``x`` in some open interval containing ``c``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nLet ``f\\left(c\\right)>0``.\n\nThen, corresponding to ``\\displaystyle\\varepsilon = \\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}>0`` there exists a corresponding ``\\delta>0`` such that ``\\left|x-c\\right|<\\delta`` implies\n```math\n\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)\\right|<\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2} \\iff -\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}<f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)<\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2} \\iff 0<\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}<f\\left(x\\right)<\\frac{3f\\left(c\\right)}{2}\\,.\n```\nHence, ``f\\left(x\\right)>0`` for all ``x\\in\\left]c-\\delta,c+\\delta\\right[``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! exercise \"Prove the second part of the Aura theorem.\"\n\nWe will first prove a special case from which the general case follows easily.\n\n::: theorem \"Bolzano's theorem\"\nLet ``f`` be a continuous function defined on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``. If ``f\\left(a\\right)<0`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)>0``, then there exists ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n:::\n\n::: proof \"by contradiction\"\nLet ``I_0=\\left[a_0, b_0\\right]=\\left[a,b\\right]``. \n\nIf ``\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2}\\right)=0``, we are done. Otherwise, ``f`` changes sign on either ``\\displaystyle\\left[a_0,\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2}\\right]`` or ``\\displaystyle\\left[\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2},b_0\\right]``.\n\nLet ``I_1=\\left[a_1, b_1\\right]`` be the subinterval on which ``f`` changes sign and repeat.\n\nBy the Nested Intervals theorem, ``\\bigcap_{n\\in \\mathbb{N}}I_n=\\left\\{c\\right\\}``, where ``c=\\sup\\left\\{a_n\\right\\}=\\inf\\left\\{b_n\\right\\}``.\n\nSuppose ``f\\left(c\\right)>0``. By the Aura theorem, ``f`` must be positive on an open interval containing ``c``. Since ``c=\\sup\\left\\{a_n\\right\\}``, by the Capture theorem this open interval must contain some ``a_m``. But ``f\\left(a_m\\right)<0`` which is a contradiction.\n\nSuppose ``f\\left(c\\right)<0``. By the Aura theorem, ``f`` must be negative on an open interval containing ``c``. Since ``c=\\inf\\left\\{b_n\\right\\}``, by the Capture theorem this open interval must contain some ``b_m``. But ``f\\left(b_m\\right)>0`` which is a contradiction.\n\nHence, ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n:::\n\nIf ``f\\left(a\\right)>0`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)<0``, then ``g=-f`` satisfies the hypotheses of Bolzano's theorem and therefore ``g\\left(c\\right)=-f\\left(c\\right)=0`` for some ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[``, and hence ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n\n::: theorem \"Intermediate-value theorem\"\nIf ``f`` is continuous on the interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` and if ``s`` is a number between ``f\\left(a\\right)`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)``, then there exists a number ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=s``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\nLet ``s`` be a number between ``f\\left(a\\right)`` and ``f\\left(b\\right)``.\n\nThe function ``g\\left(x\\right)=f\\left(x\\right)-s`` is continuous and satifies the hypotheses of Bolzano's theorem, so there exists some ``c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[`` such that ``g\\left(c\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)-s=0``.\n\nHence, ``f\\left(c\\right)=s``.\n:::\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Show that the equation ``x^3-x-1=0`` has a solution in the interval ``\\left[1,2\\right]``.\"\n\n    The function ``f\\left(x\\right)=x^3-x-1`` is a polynomial and is therefore continuous everywhere.\n\n    Now ``f\\left(1\\right)=-1`` and ``f\\left(2\\right)=5``. Since ``0`` lies between ``-1`` and ``5``, the Intermediate-value theorem assures us that there must be a number ``c\\in\\left[1,2\\right]`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=0``.\n\nOne method for finding a zero of a function that is continuous and changes sign on an interval involves bisecting the interval many times, each time determining which half of the previous interval must contain the root, because the function has opposite signs at the two ends of that half.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! example \"Solve the equation ``x^3-x-1=0`` correct to ``3`` decimal places by successive bisection.\"\n\n    The root is ``1.325`` rounded to ``3`` decimal places.","resultHTML":"<p>The figure shows a typical situation. The points <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(a, f\\left(a\\right)\\right)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(b, f\\left(b\\right)\\right)\\)</span> are on opposite sides of the horizontal line <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=s\\)</span>. Being unbroken, the graph <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> must cross this line in order to go from one point to the other. In the figure, it crosses the line only once, at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=c\\)</span>. If the line <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=s\\)</span> were somewhat lower or higher, there might have been three crossings and three possible values for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>We need the following lemma to prove the intermediate-value property.</p>\n<div data-type=\"lemma\">\n<h5>Aura theorem</h5>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> be continuous at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)&gt;0\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> in some open interval containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)&lt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)&lt;0\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> in some open interval containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)&gt;0\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Then, corresponding to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\varepsilon = \\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}&gt;0\\)</span> there exists a corresponding <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|x-c\\right|&lt;\\delta\\)</span> implies</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)\\right|&lt;\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2} \\iff -\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}&lt;f\\left(x\\right)-f\\left(c\\right)&lt;\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2} \\iff 0&lt;\\frac{f\\left(c\\right)}{2}&lt;f\\left(x\\right)&lt;\\frac{3f\\left(c\\right)}{2}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Hence, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)&gt;0\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\in\\left]c-\\delta,c+\\delta\\right[\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"exercise\">\n<h5>Prove the second part of the Aura theorem.</h5>\n</aside>\n<p>We will first prove a special case from which the general case follows easily.</p>\n<div data-type=\"theorem\">\n<h5>Bolzano’s theorem</h5>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> be a continuous function defined on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(a\\right)&lt;0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(b\\right)&gt;0\\)</span>, then there exists <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)=0\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<h5>by contradiction</h5>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I_0=\\left[a_0, b_0\\right]=\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2}\\right)=0\\)</span>, we are done. Otherwise, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> changes sign on either <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\left[a_0,\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2}\\right]\\)</span> or <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\left[\\frac{a_0+b_0}{2},b_0\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I_1=\\left[a_1, b_1\\right]\\)</span> be the subinterval on which <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> changes sign and repeat.</p>\n<p>By the Nested Intervals theorem, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bigcap_{n\\in \\mathbb{N}}I_n=\\left\\{c\\right\\}\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c=\\sup\\left\\{a_n\\right\\}=\\inf\\left\\{b_n\\right\\}\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)&gt;0\\)</span>. By the Aura theorem, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> must be positive on an open interval containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c=\\sup\\left\\{a_n\\right\\}\\)</span>, by the Capture theorem this open interval must contain some <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a_m\\)</span>. But <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(a_m\\right)&lt;0\\)</span> which is a contradiction.</p>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)&lt;0\\)</span>. By the Aura theorem, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> must be negative on an open interval containing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c=\\inf\\left\\{b_n\\right\\}\\)</span>, by the Capture theorem this open interval must contain some <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(b_m\\)</span>. But <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(b_m\\right)&gt;0\\)</span> which is a contradiction.</p>\n<p>Hence, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)=0\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(a\\right)&gt;0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(b\\right)&lt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g=-f\\)</span> satisfies the hypotheses of Bolzano’s theorem and therefore <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(c\\right)=-f\\left(c\\right)=0\\)</span> for some <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[\\)</span>, and hence <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)=0\\)</span>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"theorem\">\n<h5>Intermediate-value theorem</h5>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continuous on the interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> and if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(s\\)</span> is a number between <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(a\\right)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(b\\right)\\)</span>, then there exists a number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)=s\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(s\\)</span> be a number between <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(a\\right)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(b\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(x\\right)=f\\left(x\\right)-s\\)</span> is continuous and satifies the hypotheses of Bolzano’s theorem, so there exists some <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\in\\left]a,b\\right[\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(c\\right)=f\\left(c\\right)-s=0\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Hence, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)=s\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Show that the equation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x^3-x-1=0\\)</span> has a solution in the interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[1,2\\right]\\)</span>.</h5>\n<p>The function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)=x^3-x-1\\)</span> is a polynomial and is therefore continuous everywhere.</p>\n<p>Now <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(1\\right)=-1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(2\\right)=5\\)</span>. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span> lies between <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(5\\)</span>, the Intermediate-value theorem assures us that there must be a number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\in\\left[1,2\\right]\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)=0\\)</span>.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>One method for finding a zero of a function that is continuous and changes sign on an interval involves bisecting the interval many times, each time determining which half of the previous interval must contain the root, because the function has opposite signs at the two ends of that half.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Solve the equation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x^3-x-1=0\\)</span> correct to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(3\\)</span> decimal places by successive bisection.</h5>\n<p>The root is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1.325\\)</span> rounded to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(3\\)</span> decimal places.</p>\n</aside>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"let a = 1;\nlet b = 2;\nconst f = (x) => x ** 3 - x - 1;\nconst headers = [\n    JSCells.latex(\"i\"),\n    JSCells.latex(String.raw`a_i`),\n    JSCells.latex(String.raw`b_i`),\n    JSCells.latex(String.raw`\\displaystyle\\frac{a_i+b_i}{2}`),\n    JSCells.latex(String.raw`\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{a_i+b_i}{2}\\right)`),\n];\nconst rows = [];\n\nfor (let i = 0; i <= 11; i += 1) {\n    const m = 0.5 * (a + b);\n    const fm = f(m);\n\n    rows.push(\n        [i, a, b, m, String(Number(fm.toFixed(4)))].map((value) =>\n            JSCells.latex(String(value)),\n        ),\n    );\n\n    if (fm < 0) a = m;\n    else b = m;\n}\n\nJSCells.table({\n    headers,\n    rows,\n    align: [\"left\", \"left\", \"left\", \"left\", \"center\"],\n});","output":"","result":"iaibiai+bi2f(ai+bi2)0121.50.875111.51.25−0.296921.251.51.3750.224631.251.3751.3125−0.051541.31251.3751.343750.082651.31251.343751.3281250.014661.31251.3281251.3203125−0.018771.32031251.3281251.32421875−0.002181.324218751.3281251.3261718750.006291.324218751.3261718751.32519531250.002101.324218751.32519531251.324707031250111.324707031251.32519531251.3249511718750.001","resultHTML":"<figure data-type=\"table\"><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mi>i</mi></math></span></th><th><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span></th><th><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span></th><th><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></math></span></th><th class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></math></span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.5</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.875</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.5</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.25</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>0.2969</mn></mrow></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.25</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.5</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.375</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.2246</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>3</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.25</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.375</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3125</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>0.0515</mn></mrow></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>4</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.375</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.34375</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.0826</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>5</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.34375</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.328125</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.0146</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>6</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.328125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3203125</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>0.0187</mn></mrow></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>7</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3203125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.328125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.32421875</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>0.0021</mn></mrow></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>8</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.32421875</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.328125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.326171875</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.0062</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>9</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.32421875</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.326171875</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3251953125</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.002</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>10</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.32421875</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3251953125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.32470703125</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>11</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.32470703125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.3251953125</mn></math></span></td><td><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>1.324951171875</mn></math></span></td><td class=\"center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>0.001</mn></math></span></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"This method is slow. For example, if the original interval has length ``1``, it will take ``11`` bisections to cut down to an interval of length less than ``0.0005`` (because ``2^{11}>2000=\\frac{1}{0.0005}``, and thus to ensure that we have found the root correct to ``3`` decimal places.\n\nIn chapter 5, calculus will provide us with much faster methods of solving equations such as the one in the example above. ","output":"","result":"This method is slow. For example, if the original interval has length ``1``, it will take ``11`` bisections to cut down to an interval of length less than ``0.0005`` (because ``2^{11}>2000=\\frac{1}{0.0005}``, and thus to ensure that we have found the root correct to ``3`` decimal places.\n\nIn chapter 5, calculus will provide us with much faster methods of solving equations such as the one in the example above. ","resultHTML":"<p>This method is slow. For example, if the original interval has length <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1\\)</span>, it will take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(11\\)</span> bisections to cut down to an interval of length less than <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0.0005\\)</span> (because <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2^{11}&gt;2000=\\frac{1}{0.0005}\\)</span>, and thus to ensure that we have found the root correct to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(3\\)</span> decimal places.</p>\n<p>In chapter 5, calculus will provide us with much faster methods of solving equations such as the one in the example above.</p>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The Extreme-Value Theorem\n\nThe second of the properties states that a function ``f`` that is continuous on a closed, finite interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` must have an absolute maximum value and an absolute minimum value. This means that the values of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` at all points of the interval lie between the values of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` at two particular points in the interval; the graph of ``f`` has a highest point and a lowest point.\n\nTo prove the extreme-value theorem, we will first show that a continuous function on a closed interval is bounded; that is, there exists a constant ``K`` such that ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\le K`` if ``a\\le x\\le b``.\n\n::: theorem \"Boundedness theorem\"\nIf ``f`` is continous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``f`` is bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\n\nFor each ``x \\in \\left[a, b\\right]``, since ``f`` is continuous at ``x``, there exists an open interval ``I_x=\\left]x-\\delta_x, x+\\delta_x\\right[`` such that for all ``y \\in I_x \\cap \\left[a, b\\right]`` we have ``\\left|f\\left(y\\right) - f\\left(x\\right)\\right| < 1``.\n\nThe set of all such open intervals ``I_x`` forms an open cover of ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nBy the Heine-Borel theorem there exists a finite subcover such that ``\\left[a,b\\right]\\subset\\bigcup_{i=0}^nI_{x_i}``.\n\nFor any point ``y \\in \\left[a, b\\right]``, ``y`` must be in at least one of the intervals ``I_{x_i}=\\left]x_i-\\delta_{x_i}, x_i+\\delta_{x_i}\\right[``. Let's say ``y\\in\\left]x_j-\\delta_{x_j}, x_j+\\delta_{x_j}\\right[``.\n\nBy the property of the open interval ``\\left]x_j-\\delta_{x_j}, x_j+\\delta_{x_j}\\right[``, we know that ``\\left|f\\left(y\\right) - f\\left(x_j\\right)\\right| < 1``, i.e. ``f\\left(y\\right) < f\\left(x_j\\right) + 1`` and ``f\\left(y\\right) > f\\left(x_j\\right) - 1``.\n\nLet ``M = \\max\\left\\{f\\left(x_i\\right)|0\\le i\\le n\\right\\}+ 1`` and ``m = \\min\\left\\{f\\left(x_i\\right)|0\\le i\\le n\\right\\}- 1``.\n\nWe can conclude that for all ``y \\in \\left[a, b\\right]`` we have ``m < f\\left(y\\right) < M``.\n:::\n\nTherefore, ``f`` is bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, with ``m`` as a lower bound and ``M`` as an upper bound.\n\nFinally, we can prove the Extreme-value theorem.\n\n::: theorem \"Extreme-value theorem\"\nA continuous function on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` attains both an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n:::\n\n::: proof \"by contradiction\"\nWe prove ``f`` has a maximum on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nSince ``f`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, by the Boundedness theorem ``f`` is bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nSince ``f`` is bounded, its image set is a nonempty subset of ``\\mathbb{R}`` which is bounded above, so by the Completeness axiom it has a least upper bound.\n\nLet ``M=\\sup f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)``. By definition of ``M``, ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le M`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nSuppose that ``f\\left(x\\right)< M`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nThen ``\\displaystyle g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{M-f\\left(x\\right)}`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` and hence bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` by the Boundedness theorem.\n\nSo, there exists ``K>0`` such that ``\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{M-f\\left(x\\right)}\\le K`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nIt follows that ``\\displaystyle f\\left(x\\right)\\le M-\\frac{1}{K}`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``, which says that ``\\displaystyle M-\\frac{1}{K}`` is an upper bound for ``f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)``.\n\nSince ``K>0``, ``\\displaystyle M-\\frac{1}{K}< M``. This contradicts the fact that ``M=\\sup f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)``.\n\nHence, there must exist ``c\\in\\left[a,b\\right]`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=M``.\n:::\n\nTo see that ``f`` has a minimum on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, just note that ``h=-f`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` and therefore has a maximum of ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! exercise \"Complete the proof that a continuous function ``f`` on a closed interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` attains its absolute minimum.\"\n\nThe conclusion of the Extreme-value theorem may fail if the function ``f`` is not continuous or if the interval is not closed.","output":"","result":"## The Extreme-Value Theorem\n\nThe second of the properties states that a function ``f`` that is continuous on a closed, finite interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` must have an absolute maximum value and an absolute minimum value. This means that the values of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` at all points of the interval lie between the values of ``f\\left(x\\right)`` at two particular points in the interval; the graph of ``f`` has a highest point and a lowest point.\n\nTo prove the extreme-value theorem, we will first show that a continuous function on a closed interval is bounded; that is, there exists a constant ``K`` such that ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\le K`` if ``a\\le x\\le b``.\n\n::: theorem \"Boundedness theorem\"\nIf ``f`` is continous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``f`` is bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n:::\n\n::: proof\n\nFor each ``x \\in \\left[a, b\\right]``, since ``f`` is continuous at ``x``, there exists an open interval ``I_x=\\left]x-\\delta_x, x+\\delta_x\\right[`` such that for all ``y \\in I_x \\cap \\left[a, b\\right]`` we have ``\\left|f\\left(y\\right) - f\\left(x\\right)\\right| < 1``.\n\nThe set of all such open intervals ``I_x`` forms an open cover of ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nBy the Heine-Borel theorem there exists a finite subcover such that ``\\left[a,b\\right]\\subset\\bigcup_{i=0}^nI_{x_i}``.\n\nFor any point ``y \\in \\left[a, b\\right]``, ``y`` must be in at least one of the intervals ``I_{x_i}=\\left]x_i-\\delta_{x_i}, x_i+\\delta_{x_i}\\right[``. Let's say ``y\\in\\left]x_j-\\delta_{x_j}, x_j+\\delta_{x_j}\\right[``.\n\nBy the property of the open interval ``\\left]x_j-\\delta_{x_j}, x_j+\\delta_{x_j}\\right[``, we know that ``\\left|f\\left(y\\right) - f\\left(x_j\\right)\\right| < 1``, i.e. ``f\\left(y\\right) < f\\left(x_j\\right) + 1`` and ``f\\left(y\\right) > f\\left(x_j\\right) - 1``.\n\nLet ``M = \\max\\left\\{f\\left(x_i\\right)|0\\le i\\le n\\right\\}+ 1`` and ``m = \\min\\left\\{f\\left(x_i\\right)|0\\le i\\le n\\right\\}- 1``.\n\nWe can conclude that for all ``y \\in \\left[a, b\\right]`` we have ``m < f\\left(y\\right) < M``.\n:::\n\nTherefore, ``f`` is bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, with ``m`` as a lower bound and ``M`` as an upper bound.\n\nFinally, we can prove the Extreme-value theorem.\n\n::: theorem \"Extreme-value theorem\"\nA continuous function on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` attains both an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n:::\n\n::: proof \"by contradiction\"\nWe prove ``f`` has a maximum on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nSince ``f`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, by the Boundedness theorem ``f`` is bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nSince ``f`` is bounded, its image set is a nonempty subset of ``\\mathbb{R}`` which is bounded above, so by the Completeness axiom it has a least upper bound.\n\nLet ``M=\\sup f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)``. By definition of ``M``, ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le M`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nSuppose that ``f\\left(x\\right)< M`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nThen ``\\displaystyle g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{M-f\\left(x\\right)}`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` and hence bounded on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` by the Boundedness theorem.\n\nSo, there exists ``K>0`` such that ``\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{M-f\\left(x\\right)}\\le K`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\nIt follows that ``\\displaystyle f\\left(x\\right)\\le M-\\frac{1}{K}`` for all ``x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]``, which says that ``\\displaystyle M-\\frac{1}{K}`` is an upper bound for ``f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)``.\n\nSince ``K>0``, ``\\displaystyle M-\\frac{1}{K}< M``. This contradicts the fact that ``M=\\sup f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)``.\n\nHence, there must exist ``c\\in\\left[a,b\\right]`` such that ``f\\left(c\\right)=M``.\n:::\n\nTo see that ``f`` has a minimum on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, just note that ``h=-f`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` and therefore has a maximum of ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\n{.aside}\n!!! exercise \"Complete the proof that a continuous function ``f`` on a closed interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]`` attains its absolute minimum.\"\n\nThe conclusion of the Extreme-value theorem may fail if the function ``f`` is not continuous or if the interval is not closed.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_Lecture03-c2673-the-extremevalue-theorem\">\n<h1>The Extreme-Value Theorem</h1>\n<p>The second of the properties states that a function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> that is continuous on a closed, finite interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> must have an absolute maximum value and an absolute minimum value. This means that the values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> at all points of the interval lie between the values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> at two particular points in the interval; the graph of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has a highest point and a lowest point.</p>\n<p>To prove the extreme-value theorem, we will first show that a continuous function on a closed interval is bounded; that is, there exists a constant <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(x\\right)\\right|\\le K\\)</span> if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\le x\\le b\\)</span>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"theorem\">\n<h5>Boundedness theorem</h5>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continous on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is bounded on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<p>For each <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x \\in \\left[a, b\\right]\\)</span>, since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continuous at <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>, there exists an open interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I_x=\\left]x-\\delta_x, x+\\delta_x\\right[\\)</span> such that for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y \\in I_x \\cap \\left[a, b\\right]\\)</span> we have <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(y\\right) - f\\left(x\\right)\\right| &lt; 1\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>The set of all such open intervals <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I_x\\)</span> forms an open cover of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>By the Heine-Borel theorem there exists a finite subcover such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\subset\\bigcup_{i=0}^nI_{x_i}\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>For any point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y \\in \\left[a, b\\right]\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y\\)</span> must be in at least one of the intervals <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I_{x_i}=\\left]x_i-\\delta_{x_i}, x_i+\\delta_{x_i}\\right[\\)</span>. Let’s say <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y\\in\\left]x_j-\\delta_{x_j}, x_j+\\delta_{x_j}\\right[\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>By the property of the open interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left]x_j-\\delta_{x_j}, x_j+\\delta_{x_j}\\right[\\)</span>, we know that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left|f\\left(y\\right) - f\\left(x_j\\right)\\right| &lt; 1\\)</span>, i.e. <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(y\\right) &lt; f\\left(x_j\\right) + 1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(y\\right) &gt; f\\left(x_j\\right) - 1\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M = \\max\\left\\{f\\left(x_i\\right)|0\\le i\\le n\\right\\}+ 1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m = \\min\\left\\{f\\left(x_i\\right)|0\\le i\\le n\\right\\}- 1\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>We can conclude that for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y \\in \\left[a, b\\right]\\)</span> we have <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m &lt; f\\left(y\\right) &lt; M\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Therefore, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is bounded on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m\\)</span> as a lower bound and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M\\)</span> as an upper bound.</p>\n<p>Finally, we can prove the Extreme-value theorem.</p>\n<div data-type=\"theorem\">\n<h5>Extreme-value theorem</h5>\n<p>A continuous function on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> attains both an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"proof\">\n<h5>by contradiction</h5>\n<p>We prove <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has a maximum on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is continuous on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, by the Boundedness theorem <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is bounded on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is bounded, its image set is a nonempty subset of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb{R}\\)</span> which is bounded above, so by the Completeness axiom it has a least upper bound.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M=\\sup f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)\\)</span>. By definition of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\le M\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Suppose that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)&lt; M\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle g\\left(x\\right)=\\frac{1}{M-f\\left(x\\right)}\\)</span> is continuous on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> and hence bounded on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> by the Boundedness theorem.</p>\n<p>So, there exists <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K&gt;0\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{M-f\\left(x\\right)}\\le K\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>It follows that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle f\\left(x\\right)\\le M-\\frac{1}{K}\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\in\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, which says that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle M-\\frac{1}{K}\\)</span> is an upper bound for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K&gt;0\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle M-\\frac{1}{K}&lt; M\\)</span>. This contradicts the fact that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M=\\sup f\\left(\\left[a,b\\right]\\right)\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Hence, there must exist <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\in\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)=M\\)</span>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>To see that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> has a minimum on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, just note that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(h=-f\\)</span> is continuous on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> and therefore has a maximum of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"exercise\">\n<h5>Complete the proof that a continuous function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> on a closed interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span> attains its absolute minimum.</h5>\n</aside>\n<p>The conclusion of the Extreme-value theorem may fail if the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is not continuous or if the interval is not closed.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 60;\nconst width = 10 * scale;\nconst height = 2.75 * scale;\nconst ymid = 2.25 * scale;\nconst fontX = 14;\nconst fontY = 22;\nconst red = \"#ff0000\";\nconst black = \"#000000\";\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width,\n    height,\n    wide: true,\n    caption:\n        \"1. \\\\(f_1(x)=\\\\frac{1}{x}\\\\) is continuous on \\\\((0,1)\\\\), unbounded, and has neither a maximum nor a minimum. \" +\n        \"2. \\\\(f_2(x)=x\\\\) is continuous on \\\\((0,1)\\\\), bounded, but has neither. \" +\n        \"3. \\\\(f_3\\\\) is defined on \\\\([0,1]\\\\) but discontinuous at \\\\(x=1\\\\); it has a minimum but no maximum. \" +\n        \"4. \\\\(f_4\\\\) is discontinuous at an interior point of \\\\([0,1]\\\\); it is bounded but has neither maximum nor minimum.\",\n});\n\nfunction line(x1, y1, x2, y2, color = black) {\n    figure.line().x1(x1).y1(y1).x2(x2).y2(y2).stroke(color);\n}\n\nfunction point(xmid, x, y, fill) {\n    figure.circle()\n        .cx(xmid + scale * x)\n        .cy(ymid - scale * y)\n        .r(3)\n        .fill(fill)\n        .stroke(red);\n}\n\nfunction plot(xmid, f, start, end, samples, dots = []) {\n    const points = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < samples; i += 1) {\n        const x = start + (end - start) * i / (samples - 1);\n        points.push(`${xmid + scale * x},${ymid - scale * f(x)}`);\n    }\n    figure.polyline().points(points.join(\" \")).fill(\"none\").stroke(red).strokeWidth(1);\n    for (const x of dots) point(xmid, x, f(x), red);\n}\n\nfunction axes(left, xmid, ticks) {\n    const right = left + 2 * scale;\n\n    line(left, ymid, right - 3, ymid);\n    figure.polygon()\n        .points(`${right},${ymid} ${right - 7},${ymid - 4} ${right - 7},${ymid + 4}`)\n        .fill(black);\n\n    line(xmid, height, xmid, 3);\n    figure.polygon()\n        .points(`${xmid},0 ${xmid - 4},7 ${xmid + 4},7`)\n        .fill(black);\n\n    figure.label(\"O\", { x: xmid - 16, y: ymid, latex: true, width: fontX, height: fontY });\n    figure.label(\"x\", { x: right - 16, y: ymid - 20, latex: true, width: fontX, height: fontY });\n    figure.label(\"y\", { x: xmid + 2, y: -2, latex: true, width: fontX, height: fontY });\n\n    for (const tick of ticks) {\n        const tickX = xmid + scale * tick;\n        const label = String(tick);\n        const labelWidth = fontX * label.length;\n        line(tickX, ymid - 3, tickX, ymid + 3);\n        figure.label(label, {\n            x: tickX - labelWidth / 2,\n            y: ymid,\n            latex: true,\n            width: labelWidth,\n            height: fontY,\n        });\n    }\n}\n\nfunction curveLabel(xmid, index) {\n    figure.label(String.raw`y=f_${index}\\left(x\\right)`, {\n        x: xmid + scale - 2 * fontX,\n        y: 0,\n        width: 5 * fontX,\n        height: fontY + 2,\n        latex: true,\n    });\n}\n\nconst xOrigins = [0.5, 3, 5.5, 8].map((value) => value * scale);\nconst leftEdges = [0, 2.5, 5, 7.5].map((value) => value * scale);\n\nfor (let i = 0; i < xOrigins.length; i += 1) {\n    axes(leftEdges[i], xOrigins[i], i === 3 ? [0.5, 1] : [1]);\n}\n\n// f1(x) = 1/x on (0,1), with an open endpoint at x=1.\nplot(xOrigins[0], (x) => 1 / x, 0.1, 1, 91);\ncurveLabel(xOrigins[0], 1);\npoint(xOrigins[0], 1, 1, \"#ffffff\");\n\n// f2(x) = x on (0,1), open at both endpoints.\nplot(xOrigins[1], (x) => x, 0, 1, 101);\ncurveLabel(xOrigins[1], 2);\npoint(xOrigins[1], 0, 0, \"#ffffff\");\npoint(xOrigins[1], 1, 1, \"#ffffff\");\n\n// f3(x) = x, with f3(0)=0 and f3(1)=0.\nplot(xOrigins[2], (x) => x, 0, 1, 101, [0]);\ncurveLabel(xOrigins[2], 3);\npoint(xOrigins[2], 1, 0, red);\npoint(xOrigins[2], 1, 1, \"#ffffff\");\n\n// f4 has a jump at x=0.5.\nplot(xOrigins[3], (x) => 1 + 2 * x, 0, 0.5, 51, [0]);\nplot(xOrigins[3], (x) => 2 * x - 1, 0.5, 1, 51, [1]);\ncurveLabel(xOrigins[3], 4);\npoint(xOrigins[3], 0.5, 2, \"#ffffff\");\npoint(xOrigins[3], 0.5, 0, \"#ffffff\");\npoint(xOrigins[3], 0.5, 1, red);\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"Oxy1Oxy1Oxy1Oxy0.51y=f1(x)y=f2(x)y=f3(x)y=f4(x)1. f1(x)=1x is continuous on (0,1), unbounded, and has neither a maximum nor a minimum. 2. f2(x)=x is continuous on (0,1), bounded, but has neither. 3. f3 is defined on [0,1] but discontinuous at x=1; it has a minimum but no maximum. 4. f4 is discontinuous at an interior point of [0,1]; it is bounded but has neither maximum nor minimum.","resultHTML":"<figure class=\"wide\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"600\" height=\"165\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 165\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"117\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"120,135 113,131 113,139\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"3\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"30,0 26,7 34,7\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"14\" y=\"135\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"104\" y=\"115\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"32\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"138\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"83\" y=\"135\" width=\"14\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"267\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"270,135 263,131 263,139\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"3\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"180,0 176,7 184,7\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\